{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018v8Gcdse3Q5hdsnfcAeYbS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information needed. Let me create a comprehensive analysis document:\n\n## Summary of MLE Rounds Scaling Analysis\n\nBased on my examination of the codebase, I've identified the root cause of the inverse scaling behavior in the MLE Rounds phase. Here are the key findings:\n\n### 1. **MLE Round Loop Structure**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:312-397`\n\nThe MLE sumcheck rounds iterate `n_max` times, where:\n```\nn_max = log2(trace_height) - l_skip\n```\n\n- **APC 0 (99 AIRs):** `log2(2^20) - 0 = 20` rounds\n- **APC 300 (623 AIRs):** `log2(2^18) - 8 = 10` rounds  \n\nSo APC 300 has 2x fewer MLE rounds, not 2.35x—this should provide a **time savings of ~2x**.\n\n### 2. **Per-Round Work: Key Functions**\n\n**CPU Path** (lines 461-577 in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/cpu.rs`):\n- `sumcheck_polys_eval()` iterates over **all active traces** in each round\n- For each trace:\n  - If `round > n_lift`: Scalar work only (line 486-498)\n  - If `round <= n_lift`: Call `sumcheck_round_poly_evals()` (line 508, 560)\n\n**GPU Path** (lines 1602-1875 in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`):\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` splits traces into **Case A** (exhausted, `round > n_lift`) and **Case B** (active, `round <= n_lift`)\n\n### 3. **The Core Scaling Issue: Non-Foldable Traces**\n\n**Definition:**\n- **\"Foldable\" traces:** `n >= 0` (trace height >= 2^l_skip)\n- **\"Non-foldable\" traces:** `n < 0` (trace height < 2^l_skip), meaning `num_y = 1` permanently\n\n**Code locations:**\n- Trace classification: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/cpu.rs:110-114`\n  ```rust\n  let n_per_trace: Vec<isize> = ctx\n      .common_main_traces()\n      .map(|(_, t)| log2_strict_usize(t.height()) as isize - l_skip as isize)\n      .collect_vec();\n  let n_max: usize = n_per_trace[0].max(0) as usize;\n  ```\n\n- GPU case split: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1665-1719` (Case A, `round > n_lift`) and `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1720-1761` (Case B, `round <= n_lift`)\n\n### 4. **Per-Round Overhead per Trace**\n\nIn each MLE round, the code does:\n\n**For every active trace (from line 1693-1707, late_eval path):**\n```rust\nlate_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n    trace_idx,\n    air_idx,\n    n_lift,\n    num_y: 1,  // <-- Fixed at 1, always scalar work\n    has_constraints,\n    has_interactions,\n    norm_factor,\n    eq_xi_ptr: eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(0),\n    sels_ptr: sels.buffer().as_ptr(),\n    prep_ptr,\n    main_ptrs_dev,\n    public_ptr: public_vals.as_ptr(),\n    eq_3bs_ptr: eq_3bs.as_ptr(),\n});\n```\n\n**For Case B traces (line 1722-1749):**\n```rust\nlet log_num_y = n_lift - round;  // Shrinks as round increases\nlet num_y = 1 << log_num_y;      // 2^(n_lift - round)\nlet interp_size = sp_deg * num_y * num_columns;  // Line 1743\n```\n\nThe interpolation work per Case B trace is: **`sp_deg * num_y * num_columns`**\n\nAt each round, per-trace overhead includes:\n1. **Context building** (non-zero fixed overhead per trace)\n2. **GPU kernel dispatch & H2D copy** of trace metadata\n3. **Batch allocation** (lines 1766-1808) with per-trace descriptor creation\n\n### 5. **Why APC 300 is Slower Despite Fewer Rounds**\n\n**APC 0 Configuration:**\n- 99 AIRs (all likely foldable or with larger `n_lift`)\n- 20 MLE rounds\n- Total traces touched: ~99 × 20 = ~1,980 trace-rounds\n\n**APC 300 Configuration:**\n- 623 AIRs (many small, non-foldable with `n < 0`)\n- 10 MLE rounds\n- Total traces touched: ~623 × 10 = ~6,230 trace-rounds\n- **Per-trace overhead amortized over fewer rounds**\n\nKey overhead per MLE round:\n- **TraceCtx construction & upload** (lines 1693-1707, 1860-1874): `O(num_traces)` work\n- **Descriptor array creation** (lines 1773-1789): `O(num_traces)` allocations and H2D\n- **Kernel dispatch overhead**: Fixed per round\n- **Batch metadata computation** (lines 1620-1761): Scales with trace count\n\n### 6. **Call Chain for Per-Round Evaluation**\n\n```\nprove_zerocheck_and_logup (mod.rs:40-438)\n  └─ for round in 1..=n_max (line 312)\n      ├─ sumcheck_polys_batch_eval (GPU) / sumcheck_polys_eval (CPU) (line 313)\n      │   ├─ Phase 1: Metadata collection (lines 1617-1762)\n      │   │   ├─ Loop over all traces (line 1639)\n      │   │   ├─ Classify into Case A or B\n      │   │   └─ Build CaseBMeta per trace (line 1745)\n      │   ├─ Phase 2: GPU batch kernel (lines 1764-1808)\n      │   │   ├─ Single interpolate_columns kernel\n      │   │   └─ All Case B traces in one call\n      │   └─ Phase 3: TraceCtx + MLE evaluation (lines 1810-1875)\n      │       ├─ Build TraceCtx per Case B trace (line 1860)\n      │       ├─ Call evaluate_zerocheck_batched (batch_mle.rs:414)\n      │       │   └─ ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new (batch_mle.rs:127)\n      │       │       ├─ Filter traces (line 132): O(num_traces)\n      │       │       ├─ Compute thread config (line 146)\n      │       │       ├─ Build block_ctxs (lines 150-162): O(num_traces * num_y)\n      │       │       ├─ Build ZerocheckCtx array (lines 167-203): O(num_traces)\n      │       │       └─ H2D copy (lines 206-208): O(num_traces)\n      │       └─ evaluate (batch_mle.rs:235)\n      │           └─ evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu_batch (batch_mle.rs:669)\n      │               └─ zerocheck_batch_eval_mle kernel call\n      ├─ fold_mle_evals (GPU) / fold_mle_evals (CPU) (line 396)\n      │   └─ fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2092)\n      │       ├─ Partition traces by height (line 2098)\n      │       ├─ Build input/output pointer arrays (lines 2103-2131): O(num_foldable)\n      │       ├─ H2D copy pointers (lines 2133-2136): O(num_foldable)\n      │       ├─ batch_fold_mle kernel (lines 2138-2147)\n      │       └─ Rebuild views (lines 2154-2176): O(num_foldable)\n      └─ (per-round work repeated)\n```\n\n### 7. **Specific Per-Trace Overhead Locations**\n\n| Operation | File:Line | Cost | Scales With |\n|-----------|-----------|------|-------------|\n| Metadata loop | mod.rs:1639 | Loop over all traces | `num_traces` |\n| CaseBMeta creation | mod.rs:1745 | Per-trace struct | `num_traces` |\n| GPU batch kernel setup | mod.rs:1773-1789 | Descriptor array + H2D | `num_traces` |\n| TraceCtx construction | mod.rs:1860 | Per-trace struct | `num_traces` |\n| ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new | batch_mle.rs:127-217 | Trace filtering, context building, H2D | `num_traces` |\n| Block context array | batch_mle.rs:150-162 | Per-trace block calculation | `num_traces * num_y` |\n| ZerocheckCtx array | batch_mle.rs:167-203 | Per-trace rule setup | `num_traces` |\n| fold_pingpong pointer arrays | mod.rs:2103-2136 | Per-foldable-trace ptr collection + H2D | `num_foldable` |\n| View reconstruction | mod.rs:2154-2176 | Per-output-matrix view setup | `num_foldable` |\n\n### 8. **Why Fewer Rounds Don't Compensate**\n\n- **APC 0:** 99 traces × 20 rounds = 1,980 trace-evaluations  \n  Per-round overhead per trace: ~constant\n  Total overhead: 1,980 × O(1) per-trace-round\n\n- **APC 300:** 623 traces × 10 rounds = 6,230 trace-evaluations  \n  Per-round overhead per trace: ~constant (but slightly higher due to batching setup)\n  Total overhead: 6,230 × O(1) per-trace-round ≈ **3.15x more trace-rounds**\n\nThe per-round **batching overhead is not amortized** because:\n1. **Metadata collection** (mod.rs:1617-1762) is still O(num_traces) every round\n2. **H2D transfers** of descriptor arrays (mod.rs:2133-2136) happen every round per trace\n3. **TraceCtx construction** (mod.rs:1860) happens every round per active trace\n4. The GPU kernel itself is well-parallelized, but **CPU-side batching setup is not**\n\n### 9. **Conclusion: Where the Overhead Is**\n\nThe inverse scaling is caused by **per-trace per-round CPU-side overhead** that isn't proportional to work performed on GPU:\n\n1. **Trace iteration & metadata** (mod.rs:1639-1761): `O(num_traces)` per round\n2. **Context building & H2D** (batch_mle.rs:150-208): `O(num_traces)` per round  \n3. **fold_pingpong pointer setup** (mod.rs:2103-2136): `O(num_foldable)` per round\n4. **Kernel dispatch frequency**: One per round, with many traces per kernel\n\nWith 623 AIRs vs 99, this overhead is **6.3x larger** but MLE rounds are only **2x fewer**, resulting in **net slowdown of ~1.4x** even when the GPU work itself is 2.35x smaller in total cells."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"01ad5b0b-fe6f-4aea-bd0e-8442f993cb84","timestamp":"2026-04-16T04:55:08.186Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm optimizing a GPU STARK prover. The MLE Rounds phase shows INVERSE scaling: it takes 116ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs) but 164ms at APC 300 (623 AIRs). This is despite APC 300 having 2.35x fewer total cells.\n\nI need to understand why MLE Rounds doesn't scale. Look at:\n\n1. The MLE sumcheck round loop — where it iterates and what determines the iteration count\n2. How per-trace work scales with trace count vs trace size \n3. The `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` or similar function that does the per-round evaluation\n4. What \"foldable\" vs \"non-foldable\" traces are and how they affect MLE round count\n5. The relationship between AIR count and total MLE work\n\nKey files are in:\n- crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/ (GPU MLE implementation)\n- crates/stark-backend/src/prover/ (core prover MLE logic)\n\nReport the specific call chain and any per-trace overhead that doesn't scale with trace size.\n\nFile paths with line numbers for all key locations.","agentId":"af4d3e1ef603a15cb","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information needed. Let me create a comprehensive analysis document:\n\n## Summary of MLE Rounds Scaling Analysis\n\nBased on my examination of the codebase, I've identified the root cause of the inverse scaling behavior in the MLE Rounds phase. Here are the key findings:\n\n### 1. **MLE Round Loop Structure**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:312-397`\n\nThe MLE sumcheck rounds iterate `n_max` times, where:\n```\nn_max = log2(trace_height) - l_skip\n```\n\n- **APC 0 (99 AIRs):** `log2(2^20) - 0 = 20` rounds\n- **APC 300 (623 AIRs):** `log2(2^18) - 8 = 10` rounds  \n\nSo APC 300 has 2x fewer MLE rounds, not 2.35x—this should provide a **time savings of ~2x**.\n\n### 2. **Per-Round Work: Key Functions**\n\n**CPU Path** (lines 461-577 in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/cpu.rs`):\n- `sumcheck_polys_eval()` iterates over **all active traces** in each round\n- For each trace:\n  - If `round > n_lift`: Scalar work only (line 486-498)\n  - If `round <= n_lift`: Call `sumcheck_round_poly_evals()` (line 508, 560)\n\n**GPU Path** (lines 1602-1875 in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`):\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` splits traces into **Case A** (exhausted, `round > n_lift`) and **Case B** (active, `round <= n_lift`)\n\n### 3. **The Core Scaling Issue: Non-Foldable Traces**\n\n**Definition:**\n- **\"Foldable\" traces:** `n >= 0` (trace height >= 2^l_skip)\n- **\"Non-foldable\" traces:** `n < 0` (trace height < 2^l_skip), meaning `num_y = 1` permanently\n\n**Code locations:**\n- Trace classification: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/logup_zerocheck/cpu.rs:110-114`\n  ```rust\n  let n_per_trace: Vec<isize> = ctx\n      .common_main_traces()\n      .map(|(_, t)| log2_strict_usize(t.height()) as isize - l_skip as isize)\n      .collect_vec();\n  let n_max: usize = n_per_trace[0].max(0) as usize;\n  ```\n\n- GPU case split: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1665-1719` (Case A, `round > n_lift`) and `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1720-1761` (Case B, `round <= n_lift`)\n\n### 4. **Per-Round Overhead per Trace**\n\nIn each MLE round, the code does:\n\n**For every active trace (from line 1693-1707, late_eval path):**\n```rust\nlate_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n    trace_idx,\n    air_idx,\n    n_lift,\n    num_y: 1,  // <-- Fixed at 1, always scalar work\n    has_constraints,\n    has_interactions,\n    norm_factor,\n    eq_xi_ptr: eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(0),\n    sels_ptr: sels.buffer().as_ptr(),\n    prep_ptr,\n    main_ptrs_dev,\n    public_ptr: public_vals.as_ptr(),\n    eq_3bs_ptr: eq_3bs.as_ptr(),\n});\n```\n\n**For Case B traces (line 1722-1749):**\n```rust\nlet log_num_y = n_lift - round;  // Shrinks as round increases\nlet num_y = 1 << log_num_y;      // 2^(n_lift - round)\nlet interp_size = sp_deg * num_y * num_columns;  // Line 1743\n```\n\nThe interpolation work per Case B trace is: **`sp_deg * num_y * num_columns`**\n\nAt each round, per-trace overhead includes:\n1. **Context building** (non-zero fixed overhead per trace)\n2. **GPU kernel dispatch & H2D copy** of trace metadata\n3. **Batch allocation** (lines 1766-1808) with per-trace descriptor creation\n\n### 5. **Why APC 300 is Slower Despite Fewer Rounds**\n\n**APC 0 Configuration:**\n- 99 AIRs (all likely foldable or with larger `n_lift`)\n- 20 MLE rounds\n- Total traces touched: ~99 × 20 = ~1,980 trace-rounds\n\n**APC 300 Configuration:**\n- 623 AIRs (many small, non-foldable with `n < 0`)\n- 10 MLE rounds\n- Total traces touched: ~623 × 10 = ~6,230 trace-rounds\n- **Per-trace overhead amortized over fewer rounds**\n\nKey overhead per MLE round:\n- **TraceCtx construction & upload** (lines 1693-1707, 1860-1874): `O(num_traces)` work\n- **Descriptor array creation** (lines 1773-1789): `O(num_traces)` allocations and H2D\n- **Kernel dispatch overhead**: Fixed per round\n- **Batch metadata computation** (lines 1620-1761): Scales with trace count\n\n### 6. **Call Chain for Per-Round Evaluation**\n\n```\nprove_zerocheck_and_logup (mod.rs:40-438)\n  └─ for round in 1..=n_max (line 312)\n      ├─ sumcheck_polys_batch_eval (GPU) / sumcheck_polys_eval (CPU) (line 313)\n      │   ├─ Phase 1: Metadata collection (lines 1617-1762)\n      │   │   ├─ Loop over all traces (line 1639)\n      │   │   ├─ Classify into Case A or B\n      │   │   └─ Build CaseBMeta per trace (line 1745)\n      │   ├─ Phase 2: GPU batch kernel (lines 1764-1808)\n      │   │   ├─ Single interpolate_columns kernel\n      │   │   └─ All Case B traces in one call\n      │   └─ Phase 3: TraceCtx + MLE evaluation (lines 1810-1875)\n      │       ├─ Build TraceCtx per Case B trace (line 1860)\n      │       ├─ Call evaluate_zerocheck_batched (batch_mle.rs:414)\n      │       │   └─ ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new (batch_mle.rs:127)\n      │       │       ├─ Filter traces (line 132): O(num_traces)\n      │       │       ├─ Compute thread config (line 146)\n      │       │       ├─ Build block_ctxs (lines 150-162): O(num_traces * num_y)\n      │       │       ├─ Build ZerocheckCtx array (lines 167-203): O(num_traces)\n      │       │       └─ H2D copy (lines 206-208): O(num_traces)\n      │       └─ evaluate (batch_mle.rs:235)\n      │           └─ evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu_batch (batch_mle.rs:669)\n      │               └─ zerocheck_batch_eval_mle kernel call\n      ├─ fold_mle_evals (GPU) / fold_mle_evals (CPU) (line 396)\n      │   └─ fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2092)\n      │       ├─ Partition traces by height (line 2098)\n      │       ├─ Build input/output pointer arrays (lines 2103-2131): O(num_foldable)\n      │       ├─ H2D copy pointers (lines 2133-2136): O(num_foldable)\n      │       ├─ batch_fold_mle kernel (lines 2138-2147)\n      │       └─ Rebuild views (lines 2154-2176): O(num_foldable)\n      └─ (per-round work repeated)\n```\n\n### 7. **Specific Per-Trace Overhead Locations**\n\n| Operation | File:Line | Cost | Scales With |\n|-----------|-----------|------|-------------|\n| Metadata loop | mod.rs:1639 | Loop over all traces | `num_traces` |\n| CaseBMeta creation | mod.rs:1745 | Per-trace struct | `num_traces` |\n| GPU batch kernel setup | mod.rs:1773-1789 | Descriptor array + H2D | `num_traces` |\n| TraceCtx construction | mod.rs:1860 | Per-trace struct | `num_traces` |\n| ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new | batch_mle.rs:127-217 | Trace filtering, context building, H2D | `num_traces` |\n| Block context array | batch_mle.rs:150-162 | Per-trace block calculation | `num_traces * num_y` |\n| ZerocheckCtx array | batch_mle.rs:167-203 | Per-trace rule setup | `num_traces` |\n| fold_pingpong pointer arrays | mod.rs:2103-2136 | Per-foldable-trace ptr collection + H2D | `num_foldable` |\n| View reconstruction | mod.rs:2154-2176 | Per-output-matrix view setup | `num_foldable` |\n\n### 8. **Why Fewer Rounds Don't Compensate**\n\n- **APC 0:** 99 traces × 20 rounds = 1,980 trace-evaluations  \n  Per-round overhead per trace: ~constant\n  Total overhead: 1,980 × O(1) per-trace-round\n\n- **APC 300:** 623 traces × 10 rounds = 6,230 trace-evaluations  \n  Per-round overhead per trace: ~constant (but slightly higher due to batching setup)\n  Total overhead: 6,230 × O(1) per-trace-round ≈ **3.15x more trace-rounds**\n\nThe per-round **batching overhead is not amortized** because:\n1. **Metadata collection** (mod.rs:1617-1762) is still O(num_traces) every round\n2. **H2D transfers** of descriptor arrays (mod.rs:2133-2136) happen every round per trace\n3. **TraceCtx construction** (mod.rs:1860) happens every round per active trace\n4. The GPU kernel itself is well-parallelized, but **CPU-side batching setup is not**\n\n### 9. **Conclusion: Where the Overhead Is**\n\nThe inverse scaling is caused by **per-trace per-round CPU-side overhead** that isn't proportional to work performed on GPU:\n\n1. **Trace iteration & metadata** (mod.rs:1639-1761): `O(num_traces)` per round\n2. **Context building & H2D** (batch_mle.rs:150-208): `O(num_traces)` per round  \n3. **fold_pingpong pointer setup** (mod.rs:2103-2136): `O(num_foldable)` per round\n4. **Kernel dispatch frequency**: One per round, with many traces per kernel\n\nWith 623 AIRs vs 99, this overhead is **6.3x larger** but MLE rounds are only **2x fewer**, resulting in **net slowdown of ~1.4x** even when the GPU work itself is 2.35x smaller in total cells."}],"totalDurationMs":75394,"totalTokens":81823,"totalToolUseCount":34,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4918,"cache_read_input_tokens":73832,"output_tokens":3072,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4918},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3072,"cache_read_input_tokens":73832,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4918,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":21,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":11,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
