{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GhmuUBp5Y5KzFznUDiBaET","type":"tool_result","content":"489\t/// protocol and implementation details.\n490\t#[instrument(skip_all)]\n491\tpub fn fractional_sumcheck_gpu<SC, TS>(\n492\t    transcript: &mut TS,\n493\t    leaves: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n494\t    alpha: EF,\n495\t    assert_zero: bool,\n496\t    mem: &mut MemTracker,\n497\t) -> Result<(FracSumcheckProof<SC>, Vec<EF>), FractionalSumcheckError>\n498\twhere\n499\t    SC: StarkProtocolConfig<EF = EF>,\n500\t    TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC>,\n501\t{\n502\t    let mut layer = leaves;\n503\t    if layer.is_empty() {\n504\t        return Ok((\n505\t            FracSumcheckProof {\n506\t                fractional_sum: (EF::ZERO, EF::ONE),\n507\t                claims_per_layer: vec![],\n508\t                sumcheck_polys: vec![],\n509\t            },\n510\t            vec![],\n511\t        ));\n512\t    };\n513\t    let total_leaves = layer.len();\n514\t    // total_rounds = l_skip + n_logup\n515\t    let total_rounds = log2_strict_usize(total_leaves);\n516\t    assert!(total_rounds > 0, \"n_logup > 0 when there are interactions\");\n517\t    // Build segment tree.\n518\t    // - We only maintain the current layer\n519\t    // - Input layer uses separate F and EF buffers to save memory\n520\t    // - First tree layer converts (F, EF) to FracExt (EF, EF)\n521\t\n522\t    // We store it in bit-reversal order for coalesced memory accesses.\n523\t    // For large N (> 1024), fuse bitrev + tree layers 0 and 1 into a single kernel pass,\n524\t    // eliminating ~1.5N global memory reads. For small N, fall back to separate operations.\n525\t    let start_layer_i = if total_leaves > 1024 {\n526\t        unsafe {\n527\t            // SAFETY: Frac<EF> has exact same memory layout and alignment as (EF, EF).\n528\t            let buf = transmute::<&DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, &DeviceBuffer<(EF, EF)>>(&layer);\n529\t            bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2(buf, total_rounds as u32, alpha)\n530\t                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::BitReversal)?;\n531\t        }\n532\t        2 // layers 0+1 already done\n533\t    } else {\n534\t        // Fallback: separate bitrev + layer 0.\n535\t        unsafe {\n536\t            let buf = transmute::<&DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, &DeviceBuffer<(EF, EF)>>(&layer);\n537\t            bit_rev_frac_ext(\n538\t                buf,\n539\t                buf,\n540\t                total_rounds as u32,\n541\t                total_leaves.try_into().unwrap(),\n542\t                1,\n543\t            )\n544\t            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::BitReversal)?;\n545\t            frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, total_leaves, false, alpha, true)\n546\t                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n547\t            use crate::cuda::logup_zerocheck::frac_add_alpha;\n548\t            let half = total_leaves / 2;\n549\t            let second_half_ptr = layer.as_mut_raw_ptr() as *mut Frac<EF>;\n550\t            let second_half_buf =\n551\t                DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::from_raw_parts(second_half_ptr.add(half), half);\n552\t            frac_add_alpha(&second_half_buf, alpha)\n553\t                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n554\t            std::mem::forget(second_half_buf);\n555\t        }\n556\t        1 // layer 0 done; continue from layer 1\n557\t    };\n558\t\n559\t    // Remaining layers: fuse consecutive pairs via two-layer kernel (~33% traffic savings).\n560\t    let mut i = start_layer_i;\n561\t    while i + 1 < total_rounds {\n562\t        let half_i1 = total_leaves >> (i + 2);\n563\t        unsafe {\n564\t            frac_build_tree_two_layers(&mut layer, half_i1)\n565\t                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n566\t        }\n567\t        i += 2;\n568\t    }\n569\t    // Remaining single layer (if odd number of layers left).\n570\t    if i < total_rounds {\n571\t        unsafe {\n572\t            frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, total_leaves >> i, false, EF::ZERO, false)\n573\t                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n574\t        }\n575\t    }\n576\t    mem.emit_metrics_with_label(\"frac_sumcheck.segment_tree\");\n577\t    mem.tracing_info(\"fractional_sumcheck_gkr: after building segment tree\");\n578\t    let mut copy_scratch = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(1);\n579\t    let root = copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n580\t    unsafe {\n581\t        frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, 2, true, EF::ZERO, false)\n582\t            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n583\t    }\n584\t    if assert_zero {\n585\t        if root.p != EF::ZERO {\n586\t            return Err(FractionalSumcheckError::NonzeroRootSum {\n587\t                p: root.p,\n588\t                q: root.q,\n589\t            });\n590\t        }\n591\t    } else {\n592\t        transcript.observe_ext(root.p);\n593\t    }\n594\t    transcript.observe_ext(root.q);\n595\t\n596\t    let mut claims_per_layer = Vec::with_capacity(total_rounds);\n597\t    let mut sumcheck_polys = Vec::with_capacity(total_rounds);\n598\t\n599\t    let first_left = copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n600\t    let first_right = copy_from_device(&layer, 1, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n601\t    claims_per_layer.push(GkrLayerClaims {\n602\t        p_xi_0: first_left.p,\n603\t        q_xi_0: first_left.q,\n604\t        p_xi_1: first_right.p,\n605\t        q_xi_1: first_right.q,\n606\t    });\n607\t    for value in [\n608\t        claims_per_layer[0].p_xi_0,\n609\t        claims_per_layer[0].q_xi_0,\n610\t        claims_per_layer[0].p_xi_1,\n611\t        claims_per_layer[0].q_xi_1,\n612\t    ] {\n613\t        transcript.observe_ext(value);\n614\t    }\n615\t    let mu_1 = transcript.sample_ext();\n616\t    let mut xi_prev = vec![mu_1];\n617\t    let mut d_sum_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(2);\n618\t\n619\t    let precompute_m_env = precompute_m_enabled();\n620\t\n621\t    // Work buffer to avoid revert operations on layer. Only needed for non-last rounds.\n622\t    // For the last round (round == total_rounds - 1), we fold in-place on layer.\n623\t    // When precompute-M is active, the first multifold folds w+1 variables at once\n624\t    // (pending r_prev + w window challenges), so the output is total_leaves >> (2 + w).\n625\t    // Fold-eval fallback rounds (rem_n < min_n) need at most 2^min_n elements.\n626\t    let max_work_size = if total_rounds > 2 {\n627\t        if precompute_m_env {\n628\t            (total_leaves >> (2 + GKR_WINDOW_SIZE)).max(1 << GKR_WINDOW_DEFAULT_MIN_N)\n629\t        } else {\n630\t            total_leaves >> 2\n631\t        }\n632\t    } else {\n633\t        0\n634\t    };\n635\t    let mut work_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(max_work_size);\n636\t    let max_tmp_buffer_capacity = if total_rounds > 1 {\n637\t        (unsafe { _frac_compute_round_temp_buffer_size((1 << (total_rounds - 1)) as u32) }) as usize\n638\t    } else {\n639\t        0\n640\t    };\n641\t    let mut tmp_block_sums = if max_tmp_buffer_capacity > 0 {\n642\t        DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_tmp_buffer_capacity)\n643\t    } else {\n644\t        DeviceBuffer::new()\n645\t    };\n646\t    let precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold = precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold();\n647\t    let precompute_m_target_blocks = precompute_m_target_blocks();\n648\t    let precompute_m_tail_tile_override = precompute_m_tail_tile_override();\n649\t    let precompute_m_min_n = precompute_m_min_n();\n650\t    let mut m_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n651\t    let mut m_partial_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n652\t    let mut eq_r_prefix_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n653\t    let mut eq_suffix_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n654\t\n655\t    for round in 1..total_rounds {\n656\t        let gkr_round_span = debug_span!(\"GKR\", round).entered();\n657\t\n658\t        // Note: frac_build_tree_layer revert is now fused into do_sumcheck_round_and_revert below.\n659\t\n660\t        debug_assert_eq!(xi_prev.len(), round);\n661\t        // eq_buffer stores eq(xi_prev[j..], x) for x in H_{xi_prev.len()-j} for\n662\t        // j=1,...,xi_prev.len()-1.\n663\t        let mut eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi(&xi_prev[1..])\n664\t            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::EvalEqHypercube)?;\n665\t\n666\t        let mut round_polys_eval = Vec::with_capacity(round);\n667\t        let mut r_vec = Vec::with_capacity(round);\n668\t        let mut pq_size = 2 << round;\n669\t\n670\t        let lambda = transcript.sample_ext();\n671\t\n672\t        let tmp_buffer_capacity =\n673\t            unsafe { _frac_compute_round_temp_buffer_size((1 << round) as u32) } as usize;\n674\t        if tmp_buffer_capacity > tmp_block_sums.len() {\n675\t            tmp_block_sums = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(tmp_buffer_capacity);\n676\t        }\n677\t\n678\t        let last_outer_round = round == total_rounds - 1;\n679\t        debug_assert!(round > 0);\n680\t        let backend = choose_round_strategy(\n681\t            round,\n682\t            precompute_m_env,\n683\t            precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold,\n684\t            precompute_m_target_blocks,\n685\t            precompute_m_tail_tile_override,\n686\t            precompute_m_min_n,\n687\t        );\n688\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"63d82366-c757-4c9e-9b41-7e3863ba265d","timestamp":"2026-04-16T05:01:43.080Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs","content":"/// protocol and implementation details.\n#[instrument(skip_all)]\npub fn fractional_sumcheck_gpu<SC, TS>(\n    transcript: &mut TS,\n    leaves: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n    alpha: EF,\n    assert_zero: bool,\n    mem: &mut MemTracker,\n) -> Result<(FracSumcheckProof<SC>, Vec<EF>), FractionalSumcheckError>\nwhere\n    SC: StarkProtocolConfig<EF = EF>,\n    TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC>,\n{\n    let mut layer = leaves;\n    if layer.is_empty() {\n        return Ok((\n            FracSumcheckProof {\n                fractional_sum: (EF::ZERO, EF::ONE),\n                claims_per_layer: vec![],\n                sumcheck_polys: vec![],\n            },\n            vec![],\n        ));\n    };\n    let total_leaves = layer.len();\n    // total_rounds = l_skip + n_logup\n    let total_rounds = log2_strict_usize(total_leaves);\n    assert!(total_rounds > 0, \"n_logup > 0 when there are interactions\");\n    // Build segment tree.\n    // - We only maintain the current layer\n    // - Input layer uses separate F and EF buffers to save memory\n    // - First tree layer converts (F, EF) to FracExt (EF, EF)\n\n    // We store it in bit-reversal order for coalesced memory accesses.\n    // For large N (> 1024), fuse bitrev + tree layers 0 and 1 into a single kernel pass,\n    // eliminating ~1.5N global memory reads. For small N, fall back to separate operations.\n    let start_layer_i = if total_leaves > 1024 {\n        unsafe {\n            // SAFETY: Frac<EF> has exact same memory layout and alignment as (EF, EF).\n            let buf = transmute::<&DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, &DeviceBuffer<(EF, EF)>>(&layer);\n            bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2(buf, total_rounds as u32, alpha)\n                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::BitReversal)?;\n        }\n        2 // layers 0+1 already done\n    } else {\n        // Fallback: separate bitrev + layer 0.\n        unsafe {\n            let buf = transmute::<&DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>, &DeviceBuffer<(EF, EF)>>(&layer);\n            bit_rev_frac_ext(\n                buf,\n                buf,\n                total_rounds as u32,\n                total_leaves.try_into().unwrap(),\n                1,\n            )\n            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::BitReversal)?;\n            frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, total_leaves, false, alpha, true)\n                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n            use crate::cuda::logup_zerocheck::frac_add_alpha;\n            let half = total_leaves / 2;\n            let second_half_ptr = layer.as_mut_raw_ptr() as *mut Frac<EF>;\n            let second_half_buf =\n                DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::from_raw_parts(second_half_ptr.add(half), half);\n            frac_add_alpha(&second_half_buf, alpha)\n                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n            std::mem::forget(second_half_buf);\n        }\n        1 // layer 0 done; continue from layer 1\n    };\n\n    // Remaining layers: fuse consecutive pairs via two-layer kernel (~33% traffic savings).\n    let mut i = start_layer_i;\n    while i + 1 < total_rounds {\n        let half_i1 = total_leaves >> (i + 2);\n        unsafe {\n            frac_build_tree_two_layers(&mut layer, half_i1)\n                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n        }\n        i += 2;\n    }\n    // Remaining single layer (if odd number of layers left).\n    if i < total_rounds {\n        unsafe {\n            frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, total_leaves >> i, false, EF::ZERO, false)\n                .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n        }\n    }\n    mem.emit_metrics_with_label(\"frac_sumcheck.segment_tree\");\n    mem.tracing_info(\"fractional_sumcheck_gkr: after building segment tree\");\n    let mut copy_scratch = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(1);\n    let root = copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n    unsafe {\n        frac_build_tree_layer(&mut layer, 2, true, EF::ZERO, false)\n            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::SegmentTree)?;\n    }\n    if assert_zero {\n        if root.p != EF::ZERO {\n            return Err(FractionalSumcheckError::NonzeroRootSum {\n                p: root.p,\n                q: root.q,\n            });\n        }\n    } else {\n        transcript.observe_ext(root.p);\n    }\n    transcript.observe_ext(root.q);\n\n    let mut claims_per_layer = Vec::with_capacity(total_rounds);\n    let mut sumcheck_polys = Vec::with_capacity(total_rounds);\n\n    let first_left = copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n    let first_right = copy_from_device(&layer, 1, &mut copy_scratch)?;\n    claims_per_layer.push(GkrLayerClaims {\n        p_xi_0: first_left.p,\n        q_xi_0: first_left.q,\n        p_xi_1: first_right.p,\n        q_xi_1: first_right.q,\n    });\n    for value in [\n        claims_per_layer[0].p_xi_0,\n        claims_per_layer[0].q_xi_0,\n        claims_per_layer[0].p_xi_1,\n        claims_per_layer[0].q_xi_1,\n    ] {\n        transcript.observe_ext(value);\n    }\n    let mu_1 = transcript.sample_ext();\n    let mut xi_prev = vec![mu_1];\n    let mut d_sum_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(2);\n\n    let precompute_m_env = precompute_m_enabled();\n\n    // Work buffer to avoid revert operations on layer. Only needed for non-last rounds.\n    // For the last round (round == total_rounds - 1), we fold in-place on layer.\n    // When precompute-M is active, the first multifold folds w+1 variables at once\n    // (pending r_prev + w window challenges), so the output is total_leaves >> (2 + w).\n    // Fold-eval fallback rounds (rem_n < min_n) need at most 2^min_n elements.\n    let max_work_size = if total_rounds > 2 {\n        if precompute_m_env {\n            (total_leaves >> (2 + GKR_WINDOW_SIZE)).max(1 << GKR_WINDOW_DEFAULT_MIN_N)\n        } else {\n            total_leaves >> 2\n        }\n    } else {\n        0\n    };\n    let mut work_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(max_work_size);\n    let max_tmp_buffer_capacity = if total_rounds > 1 {\n        (unsafe { _frac_compute_round_temp_buffer_size((1 << (total_rounds - 1)) as u32) }) as usize\n    } else {\n        0\n    };\n    let mut tmp_block_sums = if max_tmp_buffer_capacity > 0 {\n        DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_tmp_buffer_capacity)\n    } else {\n        DeviceBuffer::new()\n    };\n    let precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold = precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold();\n    let precompute_m_target_blocks = precompute_m_target_blocks();\n    let precompute_m_tail_tile_override = precompute_m_tail_tile_override();\n    let precompute_m_min_n = precompute_m_min_n();\n    let mut m_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n    let mut m_partial_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n    let mut eq_r_prefix_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n    let mut eq_suffix_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::new();\n\n    for round in 1..total_rounds {\n        let gkr_round_span = debug_span!(\"GKR\", round).entered();\n\n        // Note: frac_build_tree_layer revert is now fused into do_sumcheck_round_and_revert below.\n\n        debug_assert_eq!(xi_prev.len(), round);\n        // eq_buffer stores eq(xi_prev[j..], x) for x in H_{xi_prev.len()-j} for\n        // j=1,...,xi_prev.len()-1.\n        let mut eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi(&xi_prev[1..])\n            .map_err(FractionalSumcheckError::EvalEqHypercube)?;\n\n        let mut round_polys_eval = Vec::with_capacity(round);\n        let mut r_vec = Vec::with_capacity(round);\n        let mut pq_size = 2 << round;\n\n        let lambda = transcript.sample_ext();\n\n        let tmp_buffer_capacity =\n            unsafe { _frac_compute_round_temp_buffer_size((1 << round) as u32) } as usize;\n        if tmp_buffer_capacity > tmp_block_sums.len() {\n            tmp_block_sums = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(tmp_buffer_capacity);\n        }\n\n        let last_outer_round = round == total_rounds - 1;\n        debug_assert!(round > 0);\n        let backend = choose_round_strategy(\n            round,\n            precompute_m_env,\n            precompute_m_min_blocks_threshold,\n            precompute_m_target_blocks,\n            precompute_m_tail_tile_override,\n            precompute_m_min_n,\n        );\n","numLines":200,"startLine":489,"totalLines":1271}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JqbjYjjmv4tjCQvh3w6SHG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a complex sequential loop processing GKR rounds. The fractional sumcheck processes `total_rounds - 1` rounds (where `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)`), with each round requiring GPU kernel launches + CPU transcript operations.\n\nThe key observation is that each round MUST complete before the next starts (because the challenge `r` for round k depends on the output polynomial of round k, which is computed by the GPU kernel and then the transcript is updated).\n\nThis is the fundamental sequential bottleneck of the GKR protocol. You can't parallelize the outer loop.\n\n\nWithin each round, there are multiple GPU kernel launches that happen sequentially, and some might be independent and could potentially overlap. But given how much this code has already been optimized, I should look for fundamentally different approaches rather than trying to squeeze more performance out of the existing kernels.\n\nLet me reconsider the problem from scratch. The performance gap is significant—I need to get from 1110ms down to under 901ms with APC 300, so I'm looking for something with real impact, not incremental gains.\n\nWhat if there's overhead that scales with the number of AIRs? At APC 300 with 623 AIRs, the transcript processing during the commitment phase iterates through all of them to observe commitments and public values, which adds CPU latency. Even though Poseidon2 operations are individually fast, the cumulative effect across hundreds of AIRs might be worth investigating.\n\nGPU memory allocation overhead is another angle—with 623 AIRs, the VPMM allocation patterns become more complex, and the pool state sensitivity could be contributing to the slowdown, though previous optimization attempts haven't yielded much improvement.\n\nThe stacked PCS layout at APC 300 expands to over 100,000 columns, and the RS encoding NTT operations scale with this column count, so the constant factors in the O(N log N) complexity might be playing a role here.\n\nNow I'm looking at the per-segment timings from the profiler to see where exactly the bottleneck is, but I'm thinking about a different angle entirely—what if we could overlap the MLE rounds with the stacked reduction step? Looking at the prover flow, the MLE rounds produce opening claims that feed into stacked reduction, but there might be some parallelization opportunity between the constraint proof generation and the reduction phase.\n\nExamining the code structure, the constraint proof orchestration runs GKR first, then Round 0, then MLE rounds sequentially. The timing breakdown shows these three phases taking 358ms, 182ms, and 164ms respectively. I'm wondering if we could start Round 0 before GKR finishes, or pipeline the MLE rounds with stacked reduction somehow.\n\nBut looking at the dependencies, each phase needs the previous one's output—GKR produces claims that update the transcript, Round 0 needs those claims to compute its polynomial and sample challenge r₀, and MLE rounds depend on r₀ to fold toward the claimed values. So they're fundamentally sequential and can't be overlapped.\n\nThe real opportunity seems to be in the GKR fractional sumcheck tree-building phase itself. The nsight profiling shows the initial tree construction taking 50ms across 6 kernel instances, with subsequent layer building taking 12ms across 66 instances. That tree-building cost is where I should focus optimization efforts. The GKR algorithm's tree-building phase costs about 31ms per segment for 224M leaves, split between a fused bit-reverse and layer construction kernel, then pairwise layer builds. I'm considering whether we could eliminate the tree reverts in the fractional sumcheck's `compute_round_and_revert` kernel—it spends 70ms reverting the tree fold to restore layers for opening claims, but that revert is fundamental to the protocol's bookkeeping. Another angle is tuning the `precompute_m_enabled()` threshold parameters, since the precompute-M strategy already replaces the baseline approach with a more optimized kernel for GKR rounds.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture though, after 44 iterations the gains have plateaued—recent wins were marginal (4-6ms), and the last substantial breakthrough was 64ms back in April. We're hitting the tail of the optimization curve, so reaching another 209ms reduction would require a fundamentally different approach rather than incremental kernel refinements.\n\nMaybe the answer isn't more per-component tweaking but systemic changes instead—adjusting PCS stacking parameters like l_skip or n_stack values, tuning WHIR parameters, or rethinking the multi-stream strategy with adaptive thread allocation. Though some of these might be protocol-level changes outside the scope.\n\nLet me examine the Trace Commit phase more closely. It's running at 197ms for APC 300 versus 479ms at APC 0, which is a healthy 2.43x scaling ratio. But 197ms still represents 18% of the total STARK time excluding trace, and it encompasses multiple sub-operations that might have their own optimization opportunities.\n\nThe RS encoding step is where most of the compute happens—it needs to interpolate the MLE, perform bit reversal, and run a forward NTT across the entire stacked matrix. At APC 300 that's 106K columns versus just 3.9K at APC 0, yet the phase still scales well, so that's probably not the bottleneck.\n\nNow looking at WHIR itself—it's taking 129ms at APC 300 and 218ms at APC 0, a 1.69x ratio. This is the polynomial commitment opening proof that operates on the stacked matrix at a random evaluation point, and the scaling here is noticeably worse than Trace Commit.\n\nThe cost depends on the stacked matrix dimensions, the number of WHIR rounds tied to the security parameter, and the per-round operations like Merkle hashing and polynomial evaluation. The matrix has more columns at APC 300 but fewer rows per segment, and with 2 segments versus 5, the total dimensions after reduction shift. I need to check the WHIR implementation to see what's actually driving the cost difference.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3297,"cache_read_input_tokens":123027,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3297},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"f85f2b67-c329-4a02-bf49-2722aa56b4be"}
