{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DBE9LZ2kCku9QtH1ENvg81","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a complete picture. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Analysis of WHIR GPU Prover Scaling Behavior\n\nBased on my thorough search of the codebase, here are the key scaling factors determining WHIR's runtime and why it doesn't scale proportionally with the reduction in cells/columns:\n\n### 1. **Entry Point and Parameters** \n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (lines 62-87)\n\n- `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` is the GPU entry point\n- Takes `stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2>` - vector of committed polynomials with per-round queries\n- `m = log2(stacked_height)` is computed from the input height\n- `num_whir_rounds = (m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir` (line 169)\n- **Key insight:** Number of WHIR rounds is INDEPENDENT of total cell count; it depends only on `m` (height log) and `log_final_poly_len`\n\n---\n\n### 2. **WHIR Round Loop Structure**\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (lines 199-515)\n\nThe outer loop iterates `num_whir_rounds` times:\n```\nfor (whir_round, round_params) in whir_params.rounds.iter().enumerate()  // line 199\n    for round in 0..k_whir  // line 202 (inner sumcheck loop)\n```\n\n**Critical scaling issue:** \n- At APC 300: `m = log2(811M) ≈ 30`, `log_final_poly_len` variable\n- At APC 0: `m = log2(1.9B) ≈ 31`, same `log_final_poly_len`\n- With fixed `k_whir = 4`: `num_whir_rounds` differs by at most 1\n- **The WHIR rounds themselves scale logarithmically, NOT linearly with cells**\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Data Scaling: stacked_per_commit and μ Powers**\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (lines 103-130)\n\n```rust\nlet mut total_stacked_width = 0u32;\nfor stacked in &stacked_per_commit {\n    let layout = stacked.layout();\n    for (trace, &idx) in zip(&stacked.traces, &layout.mat_starts) {\n        packets.push(packet);\n    }\n    total_stacked_width += layout.width() as u32;  // line 118\n}\nlet mu_powers = mu.powers().take(total_stacked_width as usize).collect_vec();  // line 120\n```\n\n- **μ powers vector scales with COLUMN count, not cell count**\n- At APC 300: `total_stacked_width = 623` AIRs (many columns)\n- At APC 0: `total_stacked_width = 99` AIRs (fewer columns, wider ones)\n- The algebraic batching kernel processes this linear in width: `whir_algebraic_batch_traces()` (line 128)\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Grinding Phase: Independent of Data Size**\n**Files:** \n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/sponge.rs` (lines 261-276)\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/sponge.rs` (lines 33-55)\n\nGrinding operations are called per round:\n```rust\nlet mu_pow_witness = transcript.grind_gpu(whir_params.mu_pow_bits)  // line 92\nfolding_pow_witnesses.push(transcript.grind_gpu(whir_params.folding_pow_bits))  // line 245\nquery_phase_pow_witnesses.push(transcript.grind_gpu(whir_params.query_phase_pow_bits))  // line 362\n```\n\n**Critical finding:** Grinding is **completely independent** of polynomial size:\n- Same fixed number of grind calls per round regardless of APC config\n- At APC 300 with smaller `m`: 1-2 fewer WHIR rounds = fewer grind calls\n- At APC 0 with larger `m`: 1-2 more WHIR rounds = more grind calls\n- **This explains why the 1.69x ratio < 2x: grinding calls are nearly identical, diluting the benefit of fewer rounds**\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Merkle Tree Construction and Querying**\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (lines 318-323)\n\n```rust\nlet g_tree = MerkleTreeGpu::<F, HS::Digest>::new_with_hash::<HS::MerkleHash>(\n    DeviceMatrix::new(Arc::new(g_rs), codeword_height, D_EF),\n    1 << k_whir,  // rows_per_query = 2^k_whir (line 320)\n    true,\n)\n```\n\n- Merkle tree height: `codeword_height = 1 << (log_rs_domain_size - 1)` (line 290)\n- `rows_per_query = 2^k_whir` is fixed (line 320)\n- Tree size grows as `O(2^(log_rs_domain_size - 1))` per round\n- **Tree compression cost scales with RS domain size, not with original cell count**\n\nQuery cost:\n```rust\nlet num_queries = round_params.num_queries;  // line 358\nquery_phase_pow_witnesses.push(transcript.grind_gpu(...))  // line 362\n```\n- `num_queries` is determined by `whir_params.rounds[whir_round].num_queries` (fixed at setup time)\n- Queries per round are **independent of APC configuration**\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Per-Segment Iteration: Initial Round Overhead**\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` (lines 375-443)\n\nIn WHIR round 0 only:\n```rust\nif whir_round == 0 {\n    let mut backing_mats_owned = vec![None; stacked_per_commit.len()];  // num_commits\n    for (d, backing_mat_owned) in zip(&mut stacked_per_commit, &mut backing_mats_owned) {\n        // If backing matrix not cached, regenerate it:\n        let backing_matrix = rs_code_matrix(log_blowup, layout, &traces, &d.inner.matrix)\n        // Query merkle proofs and open rows per commit (lines 399-425)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**APC-dependent scaling:**\n- At APC 300: `num_commits = 623` separate commitments\n- At APC 0: `num_commits = 99` separate commitments\n- Initial round loops `num_commits` times for:\n  - Backing matrix generation (if not cached)\n  - Merkle proof queries per commitment\n  - Row opening per commitment\n- **More AIRs (higher APC) = more loop iterations in initial round**\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Sumcheck Coefficient/Moment Operations**\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` (lines 83-136, 138-166)\n\nPer inner round (k_whir times per WHIR round):\n```cuda\nwhir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round_kernel()  // evaluates 2 polynomials\nwhir_fold_coeffs_and_moments_kernel()       // folds coefficients\n```\n\n- `f_height = 1 << (m - round)` shrinks by 2 each inner round (line 204)\n- Operations scale **linearly with f_height** within each round\n- **Lower APC (larger m) means larger f_height for the same round index**\n\nAt APC 300: `m ≈ 30`\nAt APC 0: `m ≈ 31`\n- Inner round 0: f_height differs by 2x\n- But this is only `k_whir` rounds per WHIR round, amortized across all WHIR rounds\n\n---\n\n### **Summary: Why Scaling is Only 1.69x Instead of 2x**\n\n| Factor | APC 300 | APC 0 | Scaling |\n|--------|---------|-------|---------|\n| **m (log height)** | ~30 | ~31 | +1 bit |\n| **num_whir_rounds** | ~(30 - L) / 4 | ~(31 - L) / 4 | +0-1 rounds |\n| **Cells** | 811M | 1.9B | 2.34x |\n| **Grinding calls** | (num_whir_rounds + k_whir + 1) × 3 (μ, folding, query) | Nearly identical (+1 round) | ~1.1x |\n| **num_commits (AIRs)** | 623 | 99 | 6.3x (HIGHER at APC 300) |\n| **Initial round per-commit loops** | 623 iterations | 99 iterations | 6.3x MORE work at APC 300 |\n| **Merkle tree size per round** | Smaller | Larger | Scales with m |\n| **Sumcheck kernel ops per round** | Linear in f_height | 2x larger f_height | ~1.5-2x slower at APC 0 |\n\n**Key bottleneck:** The **initial round processes more commitments at APC 300**, which dilutes the theoretical 2x speedup from cell reduction. Additionally, grinding operations (which are data-size-independent) occupy a larger fraction of total runtime at lower cell counts, creating a floor on optimization."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"7253677e-7a71-4d25-a5b8-8de645ac2c61","timestamp":"2026-04-16T05:03:52.507Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm optimizing a GPU STARK prover. WHIR takes 129ms at APC 300 (623 AIRs, 811M cells) and 218ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, 1.9B cells). The ratio is 1.69x but should be >2x.\n\nI need to understand what determines WHIR's runtime and why it doesn't scale proportionally with the reduction in cells/columns.\n\nSearch in /home/georg/stark-backend for:\n1. The `prove_whir_opening_gpu` function — its entry point, parameters, and how it processes multip