o_host()` calls are creating unnecessary pipeline drains. I'm considering whether we could batch the transfers and do a single synchronization at the end of a round instead, but the constraint is that the CPU needs the results immediately to process them — `compute_batch_s_poly` depends on having those evaluations available, so we can't defer the host copies. The real opportunity might be launching all the kernels first without intermediate syncs, then doing one collective sync followed by copying everything to host at once.\n\nSince all kernels run on the same stream sequentially anyway, we could launch the interpolation, logup eval, zerocheck eval, and monomial eval kernels back-to-back, then sync once and copy all results. That would eliminate the overhead of four extra syncs per round — roughly 10ms across 24 rounds at 100µs per sync. It's modest savings, but worth exploring alongside other optimizations.\n\nThe tricky part is that the evaluation functions currently bundle the kernel launch with the host sync, so we'd need to refactor them to separate those concerns. Once we do that, we unlock something bigger: overlapping GPU MLE evaluation from the current round with CPU batch_s_poly computation from the previous round.\n\nThe challenge is that the fold operation at the end of each round modifies the matrices that the next round's evaluation reads, so we can't overlap those directly. But we might be able to run the CPU compute_batch_s step in parallel with the GPU fold operation instead.\n\nActually, looking at the dependencies more carefully: the fold needs `r_round` from the transcript, which itself depends on `batch_s_evals` from compute_batch_s. So there's a strict sequential chain—GPU eval → CPU compute → CPU transcript → GPU fold—that prevents any CPU-GPU parallelism here.\n\nI'm wondering if we could restructure the computation to compute `batch_s_evals` during the GPU eval phase itself, using partial results from earlier rounds, which might create an opportunity for overlap.\n\nBut that won't work since `compute_batch_s` needs the complete evaluation output. However, what if we move the batch_s computation onto the GPU entirely? The operation is essentially a weighted sum over 623 traces—multiply mu_pows by eval results and accumulate—which is a perfect fit for GPU reduction. We could fuse this into the evaluation kernel itself, accumulating the weighted sum directly in shared memory, then transfer only the final sp_deg values back to CPU instead of the full 623×sp_deg matrix. This eliminates the large D2H transfer, the CPU loop, and reduces the data movement significantly.\n\nThe tradeoff is that this requires modifying the existing kernel to accept mu_pows as input and handle the atomic accumulation. A cleaner alternative would be to keep the kernels unchanged and add a separate reduction kernel that takes the per-trace evaluations and combines them with the weights—this avoids touching the current pipeline while still capturing most of the performance gain.\n\nThe key insight is that instead of copying thousands of per-trace values back to CPU and reducing them there, I can do the reduction on the GPU and only copy back the final aggregated results. This shrinks the D2H transfer from roughly 40KB per round down to just a few hundred bytes, eliminating both the large memory transfer and the CPU reduction loop entirely. The potential speedup from reducing sync points is modest—around 7-10ms across all 24 rounds—but worth pursuing as part of a broader optimization effort. I'm settling on a concrete approach: defer D2H transfers and aggregate batch polynomials on the GPU side to eliminate the pipeline stalls caused by multiple syncs per round. The idea is to launch all per-round kernels sequentially on the same stream without intermediate transfers, write results to a contiguous device buffer, then sync once after everything completes.\n\nThat said, 20-25ms of savings feels incremental rather than transformative. 