{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DjFCu2Jh3W4qXmpezMp5JP","type":"tool_result","content":"96\t// Device -> Host\n97\tpub trait MemCopyD2H<T> {\n98\t    fn to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>;\n99\t}\n100\t\n101\timpl<T> MemCopyD2H<T> for DeviceBuffer<T> {\n102\t    fn to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError> {\n103\t        let mut host_vec = Vec::with_capacity(self.len());\n104\t        let size_bytes = std::mem::size_of::<T>() * self.len();\n105\t\n106\t        check(unsafe {\n107\t            cudaMemcpyAsync(\n108\t                host_vec.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n109\t                self.as_raw_ptr(),\n110\t                size_bytes,\n111\t                cudaMemcpyKind::cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost,\n112\t                cudaStreamPerThread,\n113\t            )\n114\t        })?;\n115\t        unsafe {\n116\t            COPY_EVENT\n117\t                .lock()\n118\t                .unwrap()\n119\t                .record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)?;\n120\t\n121\t            host_vec.set_len(self.len());\n122\t        }\n123\t\n124\t        Ok(host_vec)\n125\t    }\n126\t}\n127\t\n128\t/// Like [`MemCopyD2H`], but syncs on the calling thread's per-thread stream instead of the global\n129\t/// `COPY_EVENT` mutex. Safe to call from multiple threads without mutex contention.\n130\tpub trait MemCopyD2HStreamSync<T> {\n131\t    fn to_host_on_current_stream(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>;\n132\t}\n133\t\n134\timpl<T> MemCopyD2HStreamSync<T> for DeviceBuffer<T> {\n135\t    fn to_host_on_current_stream(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError> {\n136\t        let mut host_vec = Vec::with_capacity(self.len());\n137\t        let size_bytes = std::mem::size_of::<T>() * self.len();\n138\t        check(unsafe {\n139\t            cudaMemcpyAsync(\n140\t                host_vec.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n141\t                self.as_raw_ptr(),\n142\t                size_bytes,\n143\t                cudaMemcpyKind::cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost,\n144\t                cudaStreamPerThread,\n145\t            )\n146\t        })?;\n147\t        current_stream_sync().map_err(MemCopyError::from)?;\n148\t        unsafe {\n149\t            host_vec.set_len(self.len());\n150\t        }\n151\t        Ok(host_vec)\n152\t    }\n153\t}\n154\t\n155\tpub trait MemCopyD2D<T> {"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"b4cfce98-2e72-4b07-b156-52c915302946","timestamp":"2026-04-16T05:11:32.025Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs","content":"// Device -> Host\npub trait MemCopyD2H<T> {\n    fn to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>;\n}\n\nimpl<T> MemCopyD2H<T> for DeviceBuffer<T> {\n    fn to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError> {\n        let mut host_vec = Vec::with_capacity(self.len());\n        let size_bytes = std::mem::size_of::<T>() * self.len();\n\n        check(unsafe {\n            cudaMemcpyAsync(\n                host_vec.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n                self.as_raw_ptr(),\n                size_bytes,\n                cudaMemcpyKind::cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost,\n                cudaStreamPerThread,\n            )\n        })?;\n        unsafe {\n            COPY_EVENT\n                .lock()\n                .unwrap()\n                .record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)?;\n\n            host_vec.set_len(self.len());\n        }\n\n        Ok(host_vec)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Like [`MemCopyD2H`], but syncs on the calling thread's per-thread stream instead of the global\n/// `COPY_EVENT` mutex. Safe to call from multiple threads without mutex contention.\npub trait MemCopyD2HStreamSync<T> {\n    fn to_host_on_current_stream(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>;\n}\n\nimpl<T> MemCopyD2HStreamSync<T> for DeviceBuffer<T> {\n    fn to_host_on_current_stream(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError> {\n        let mut host_vec = Vec::with_capacity(self.len());\n        let size_bytes = std::mem::size_of::<T>() * self.len();\n        check(unsafe {\n            cudaMemcpyAsync(\n                host_vec.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n                self.as_raw_ptr(),\n                size_bytes,\n                cudaMemcpyKind::cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost,\n                cudaStreamPerThread,\n            )\n        })?;\n        current_stream_sync().map_err(MemCopyError::from)?;\n        unsafe {\n            host_vec.set_len(self.len());\n        }\n        Ok(host_vec)\n    }\n}\n\npub trait MemCopyD2D<T> {","numLines":60,"startLine":96,"totalLines":210}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BgiT7nXPCW9cVhnFmLv1P5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key finding! Both `to_host()` and `to_host_on_current_stream()` sync the stream:\n\n1. `to_host()`: uses `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `COPY_EVENT.lock().unwrap().record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)` — mutex lock + event record + wait\n2. `to_host_on_current_stream()`: uses `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `current_stream_sync()` — stream sync\n\nBoth are synchronous — they block the CPU until the GPU completes. \n\nThe key difference is that `to_host()` relies on a global mutex for synchronization while `to_host_on_current_stream()` syncs the current stream directly. For single-threaded MLE rounds, the mutex remains uncontended, so both approaches have similar latency. Each call breaks down into the async enqueue (~1µs) and the pipeline drain (~50-200µs), where the latter dominates since it waits for all prior GPU operations to finish.\n\nWhen kernels run sequentially with syncs between them, each synchronization waits only for the immediately preceding kernel rather than all of them. So if Kernel A takes 500µs, B takes 200µs, and C takes 300µs, the total time is their sum plus the overhead from each sync call itself (~10-50µs each). Across 24 rounds, that sync overhead accumulates to roughly 720-3600µs total.\n\nThe real bottleneck isn't the synchronization overhead—it's the kernel execution time itself. The sync just blocks the CPU from doing anything useful while the GPU runs. The core problem is the dependency chain within each MLE round: the GPU computes evaluation results, then the CPU must wait for that output before computing batch_s and the transcript, and only then can the GPU fold. This creates GPU idle time during the CPU work phase, which compounds across all 24 rounds.\n\nIf the CPU-side work (batch_s computation, transcript generation, and fold setup) takes around 150-200µs per round, that's roughly 4ms total overhead across all rounds—which is negligible. So the 63ms of non-kernel time must be coming from somewhere else. Let me reconsider what nsight is actually measuring here, since the GPU kernel time reported might not account for the full picture of how the GPU and CPU are interleaved. -8 kernels per round, so roughly 200 kernel instances total across all 24 MLE rounds. With each kernel averaging around 0.5ms, the inter-kernel gaps of ~0.3ms each add up to about 60ms of overhead — that's the CPU preparing descriptors, copying data to the GPU, and setting up the next launch while the GPU sits idle between kernels. At APC 300, each round chains together interpolation, logup and zerocheck evaluations (both late and early), monomial evaluation, a stream sync, and a fold operation, creating these repeated launch-to-launch delays.\n\nThe solution is to fuse multiple kernels together — combining logup and zerocheck evaluations into one kernel, merging interpolation with evaluation, and reducing the number of sub-batch launches to cut down on the CPU-side preparation overhead between each kernel.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel breakdown per round at APC 300, I see there are separate launches for late and early evaluations: interpolation, late logup eval, late zerocheck eval, early logup eval, early zerocheck eval, and monomial eval — that's around 6-7 kernels per round, which explains the cumulative overhead when multiplied across 24 rounds. low-num_y traces)\n- to_host sync\n- Fold: 1 launch (batch_fold_mle)\n- Eq tree update: 1 launch\n\nThat's ~8-9 kernel launches per round. Between each: CPU preparation (~50-200µs).\n\nNow, from nsight we see:\n- zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel: 24 instances (not 48 = 2×24)\n- logup_batch_mle_kernel: 45 instances\n\nSo zerocheck has 24 instances (1 per round) and logup has 45 instances (~2 per round — maybe late+early, or sub-batched). Some rounds might not have both late and early.\n\nBreaking down the kernel distribution across 24 rounds: zerocheck_batch_mle runs once per round, logup_batch_mle about twice, zerocheck_monomial_par_y roughly twice, zerocheck_monomial heavily sub-batched at ~7 times per round, and both batched_interpolate_columns_matrix and batched_nondegen_mle_round run about 4 times each. That adds up to roughly 20 kernel launches per round, totaling around 480 across all rounds. With inter-launch gaps of ~100µs each, that's substantial overhead just from kernel launch latency.\n\nThe 63ms overhead likely comes from these ~480 kernel launches plus synchronization costs. The real bottleneck is the sub-batching in zerocheck_monomial and the interpolate/nondegen kernels — they're being split across multiple launches per round due to memory constraints from find_batch_end. Merging those sub-batches or increasing the batch size limit would directly reduce launch count and overhead. Increase the memory budget for MLE batch kernels to reduce sub-batching**\n\nThe `find_batch_end` function (batch_mle.rs:61-86) limits the number of traces per batch based on memory budget. If we increase the budget, more traces fit per batch, fewer launches per round.\n\nCurrently, the memory budget is probably set conservatively. At APC 300, the GPU has 24GB and the MLE intermediates are relatively small per trace.\n\nIf we double the memory budget, we might halve the number of sub-batch launches:\n- zerocheck_monomial_kernel: 178 → ~89 instances\n- batched_interpolate: 93 → ~47\n- batched_nondegen_mle_round: 93 → ~47\n\nThat could save around 18ms per round. But I should verify what the current memory budget actually is and whether it's safe to increase it without running into GPU memory constraints.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3679,"cache_read_input_tokens":161605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3679},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inferenc