{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V7yu3WPmBZgquzpRjtF6Ef","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see three types of monomial batch evaluators in batch_mle_monomial.rs:\n1. `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate` — for standard monomial zerocheck\n2. `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::evaluate` — for par-Y monomial zerocheck (parallelizes over y values)\n3. `LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate` — for logup monomial\n\nEach is already batched across ALL traces of its type. So the 178 instances of `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` are from 178 calls across all rounds. \n\nAt 24 MLE rounds per proof, that's roughly 7.4 kernel instances per round. But `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate` should only be called once per round for all low-num_y traces, so the 178 instances must mean the kernel is being launched multiple times within a single evaluate() call rather than once per call.\n\nLooking at the evaluate() implementation, it allocates temporary buffers and launches the zerocheck_monomial_batched kernel in a single kernel launch per call. So 178 kernel instances would mean 178 evaluate() calls, but there should only be around 24 if it's one per MLE round. This suggests there's sub-batching happening in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval that's calling the monomial path multiple times per round. Let me trace through that code to understand where the extra calls are coming from.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1464,"cache_read_input_tokens":178775,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1464},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"152045a8-e63c-4197-92d3-ed9de7eefd91"}
