elong to the app proof versus the recursion proofs—the app proof has 2 segments with their own `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` calls, and that's where the ~24 monomial kernel calls should be concentrated.\n\nWithout NVTX markers at the kernel level, I can't precisely map which nsight instances fall within the 164ms MLE Rounds window versus the recursion proofs. I could estimate based on proportions—MLE Rounds is about 3% of the total 5.39 second benchmark—but that's too coarse. Let me shift focus to analyzing the per-round behavior instead.\n\nAcross the 2 MLE Round segments with roughly 12 rounds each, that's about 24 total rounds, giving roughly 6.8ms per round. Within each round, there are about 8 kernel launches (interpolation, late logup, late zerocheck, early logup, early zerocheck DAG, early zerocheck par-y mono, early zerocheck std mono, fold), and the inter-launch gaps plus host synchronization calls add up to roughly 1.5-1.7ms of overhead per round, with kernel execution itself taking around 4ms.\n\nOver all 24 rounds, that's roughly 36-40ms of overhead, leaving about 23ms unaccounted for in the remaining time budget. The deferred synchronization approach could potentially save around 0.5ms per round, which compounds to roughly 12ms across all rounds. I've spent enough time analyzing—let me commit to the best optimization and move forward.\n\nI'm going with deferred D2H synchronization in the MLE sumcheck evaluation. The approach is to batch all the per-round GPU kernels without intermediate host syncs, then do a single synchronization after everything completes.\n\nThis eliminates multiple intermediate stream syncs per round, potentially saving 10-15ms at APC 300. I also considered merging the late and early evaluation logup paths into a single kernel when both have traces in the same round, but the deferred sync approach offers the biggest gain with the lowest risk. Actually, let me step back and look at what the recent performance reports are actually showing for the specific timing breakdown.\n\nLooking at the multistream MLE round evaluation report, it turns out the 162ms spent in MLE Rounds is dominated by actual GPU kernel compute, not launch overhead or idle SMs. This means my earlier estimate of 63ms in \"overhead\" was likely underestimating the kernel time itself—I was probably only counting a subset of kernel instances in my nsight analysis. The previous attempt at multi-streaming MLE rounds to overlap kernel execution had no measurable impact, which confirms that MLE Rounds is genuinely compute-bound rather than launch-bound. So the ~12ms per round across 14 rounds is real kernel compute time, not something that can be optimized away through launch overhead reduction.\n\nSince MLE Rounds optimization is off the table based on these findings, I need to step back and look at which other phases haven't been confirmed as kernel-bound yet. LogUp GKR at 358ms is memory-bandwidth-bound according to the warp-tiled-gkr-intermediates report, with both input eval and fractional sumcheck being GPU-bound. Trace Commit at 197ms is GPU-bound with NTT and Merkle hashing that scales well. Round 0 at 182ms is multi-streamed and GPU-bound, with per-segment time dominated by kernel execution. WHIR at 129ms has logarithmic dependence on data size and includes some fixed costs.\n\nLooking at Stacked Reduction at 74ms, the CPU NTT dominates at around 36ms. Since all major components are either GPU-kernel-bound or CPU-bound with no significant overhead to eliminate, the remaining optimization paths are algorithmic improvements to reduce actual GPU kernel work, protocol parameter tuning, or GPU kernel optimization itself. I can't change the protocol for (a), parameter tuning might help if settings are suboptimal, and GPU kernel optimization is possible but requires deep expertise. Let me focus on GPU kernel optimization for the largest contributors—the evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel is taking 452ms globally, which is where the