{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Be8tPydTzTDnGJptbmbKqj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013bAQRCTYFCwMJYYxJYRPAY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (iDFT + polynomial multiplication + accumulation) from CPU to GPU using pre-computed transformation matrices, matching the pattern successfully used in the constraint Round 0 GPU poly extraction (task gpu-round0-poly-extraction, which saved 47ms). The current CPU-side `reconstruct_s0_from_g` / `ntt_multiply_and_add` pipeline performs per-bucket iDFT, DFT, pointwise multiplication, and inverse DFT sequentially on the host, creating a CPU bottleneck that blocks the GPU pipeline. A GPU kernel can perform the equivalent computation as a matrix-vector multiply, eliminating intermediate D2H copies and parallelizing the reconstruction.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3843,"cache_read_input_tokens":190478,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3843},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"f862044c-58b5-4c66-91f7-a438603fd650"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013bAQRCTYFCwMJYYxJYRPAY","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"4cfa146a-dbcc-48c3-b621-e50e177f241a","timestamp":"2026-04-16T05:20:17.570Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (iDFT + polynomial multiplication + accumulation) from CPU to GPU using pre-computed transformation matrices, matching the pattern successfully used in the constraint Round 0 GPU poly extraction (task gpu-round0-poly-extraction, which saved 47ms). The current CPU-side `reconstruct_s0_from_g` / `ntt_multiply_and_add` pipeline performs per-bucket iDFT, DFT, pointwise multiplication, and inverse DFT sequentially on the host, creating a CPU bottleneck that blocks the GPU pipeline. A GPU kernel can perform the equivalent computation as a matrix-vector multiply, eliminating intermediate D2H copies and parallelizing the reconstruction.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-16-0600-tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir","+# 2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction"," ","-Tune the VPMM page size to recover the 28ms WHIR regression (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) that appeared after increasing page size from 2 MiB to 16 MiB. The 16 MiB change gave a net -186ms improvement by reducing cuMemCreate overhead and routing medium allocations through cudaMallocAsync, but WHIR regressed because its 2-4 MiB buffers moved from VPMM to cudaMallocAsync. Test intermediate page sizes (4, 8, 10, 12 MiB) to find a sweet spot that preserves the large-allocation benefits while keeping WHIR buffers in VPMM. The existing VPMM_PAGE_SIZE environment variable enables rapid testing without code changes.","+Move the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (iDFT + polynomial multiplication + accumulation) from CPU to GPU using pre-computed transformation matrices, matching the pattern successfully used in the constraint Round 0 GPU poly extraction (task gpu-round0-poly-extraction, which saved 47ms). The current CPU-side `reconstruct_s0_from_g` / `ntt_multiply_and_add` pipeline performs per-bucket iDFT, DFT, pointwise multiplication, and inverse DFT sequentially on the host, creating a CPU bottleneck that blocks the GPU pipeline. A GPU kernel can perform the equivalent computation as a matrix-vector multiply, eliminating intermediate D2H copies and parallelizing the reconstruction."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-0600-tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir\n\nTune the VPMM page size to recover the 28ms WHIR regression (100ms → 128ms at APC 300) that appeared after increasing page size from 2 MiB to 16 MiB. The 16 MiB change gave a net -186ms improvement by reducing cuMemCreate overhead and routing medium allocations through cudaMallocAsync, but WHIR regressed because its 2-4 MiB buffers moved from VPMM to cudaMallocAsync. Test intermediate page sizes (4, 8, 10, 12 MiB) to find a sweet spot that preserves the large-allocation benefits while keeping WHIR buffers in VPMM. The existing VPMM_PAGE_SIZE environment variable enables rapid testing without code changes.\n"}}
