{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KyMMt9ay93emeamqu9Fe9b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction from CPU to GPU, eliminating the CPU-side iDFT + polynomial multiplication pipeline in `reconstruct_s0_from_g` / `ntt_multiply_and_add`. Currently, the accumulated G0/G1/G2 evaluations are transferred from GPU to CPU for NTT-based polynomial multiplication — an approach that incurs D2H sync latency and serializes the computation on the host. A GPU-side transformation matrix kernel can perform the equivalent computation as a matrix-vector multiply, keeping data on device until only the final ~5 s_0 coefficients need to be transferred.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n10\t\n11\tPer-trace GPU kernel accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations (on identity coset) into bucket buffers:\n12\t- `d_g_pos`: for n ≥ 0 traces — 3 × skip_domain elements\n13\t- `d_g_neg[k]`: for n < 0 traces at each depth — 3 × skip_domain elements each, up to l_skip buckets\n14\t\n15\tThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (stacked_reduction.rs:566-641) which:\n16\t\n17\t1. **D2H copies** (lines 581, 605): `d_g_pos.to_host()` and `d_g_neg_bucket.to_host()` — each syncs the stream\n18\t2. **E polynomial construction** (lines 584-586, 617-624): Compute challenge-dependent E0, E1, E2 polynomials on CPU. For n<0 buckets, multiply by indicator polynomial.\n19\t3. **ntt_multiply_and_add** (stacked_reduction.rs:645-686): For each bucket:\n20\t   - iDFT 3 G evaluation vectors (256 elements each) → G coefficients\n21\t   - Pad + transpose to (512 × 3) column-major matrices\n22\t   - DFT batch both E and G\n23\t   - Pointwise multiply (sum_i E_i × G_i) at each evaluation point\n24\t   - iDFT result → s_0 coefficients\n25\t   - Accumulate into output\n26\t\n27\t### Why it's slow\n28\t\n29\tWith l_skip=8, skip_domain=256:\n30\t- Up to 9 D2H + stream sync calls (1 for n≥0, up to 8 for n<0) at ~200µs each = ~1.8ms\n31\t- Per-bucket CPU NTT: iDFT(3×256) + DFT batch(3×512×2) + iDFT(512) = ~8-10 NTTs per bucket\n32\t- With n<0 buckets also doing indicator polynomial multiplication (3 additional NTTs each)\n33\t- Total: ~80-100 CPU NTT calls on 256-512 element vectors\n34\t- Estimated total CPU time: 15-20ms across both segments\n35\t\n36\t## Changes\n37\t\n38\t### Step 1: Pre-compute transformation matrix on CPU\n39\t\n40\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n41\t\n42\tAdd a function `compute_s0_transform_matrix` that, for a given bucket (n≥0 or specific n<0), computes the dense transformation matrix T where:\n43\t\n44\t```\n45\ts_0_coefficients = T × [G0_evals; G1_evals; G2_evals]\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tT has shape `(s_0_deg + 1) × (3 × skip_domain)` — with l_skip=8 and s_0_deg=4, this is 5 × 768 = 3840 EF elements (61 KB).\n49\t\n50\t**Construction method** (per bucket):\n51\t- For each output coefficient index `out_j` in 0..s_0_deg+1:\n52\t  - For each G index `gi` in 0..3:\n53\t    - For each eval index `k` in 0..skip_domain:\n54\t      - Compute: the contribution of `G_gi[k]` to `s_0[out_j]`\n55\t      - This equals: iDFT_weight(k→coeff) × E_gi(coeff→eval) × product_combine × iDFT_weight(eval→out_j)\n56\t      - In practice: create a unit vector with 1 at position k, run it through iDFT + multiply_by_E_gi + DFT + iDFT, read coefficient out_j\n57\t      - Store in `T[out_j][gi * skip_domain + k]`\n58\t\n59\tA more efficient construction: since the whole pipeline is linear, compute T column by column:\n60\t- For each column (gi, k): set g_evals = unit vector at position k for G_gi\n61\t- Run the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` equivalent on this unit vector\n62\t- The resulting s_0 coefficients are column (gi, k) of T\n63\t\n64\tThis takes `3 × skip_domain` NTT calls (3 × 256 = 768 unit-vector reconstructions). Each call involves small NTTs (~20µs each). Total: 768 × 20µs = ~15ms.\n65\t\n66\t**Optimization**: Compute T using direct linear algebra instead of repeated NTT:\n67\t1. Compute the iDFT matrix for skip_domain (256×256)\n68\t2. Compute E polynomial evaluation vectors\n69\t3. T = (output iDFT) × diag(E_evals) × (DFT × iDFT_G)\n70\t\n71\tThis is matrix-matrix multiplication, which can be done in ~1ms on CPU for these small dimensions. The matrices are:\n72\t- iDFT_G: skip_domain × skip_domain (256×256)\n73\t- E_i evaluation: diagonal (512×512) \n74\t- Final iDFT: (s_0_deg+1) × domain_size (5×512)\n75\t\n76\tWe compose: T_gi = Final_iDFT × diag(E_gi_evals) × pad_and_DFT × iDFT_G\n77\t\n78\tThis is computed once per bucket at proof time (depends on challenge values r_0, eq_const, omega_skip).\n79\t\n80\t### Step 2: Upload transformation matrices to GPU\n81\t\n82\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n83\t\n84\tAfter computing T matrices for all active buckets (n≥0 and each non-zero n<0):\n85\t- Concatenate into a single flat buffer\n86\t- Upload via `to_device()` — at most ~500KB for 9 buckets, negligible transfer time\n87\t\n88\t### Step 3: GPU kernel for matrix-vector multiply\n89\t\n90\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n91\t\n92\tAdd a new CUDA kernel `stacked_reduction_extract_s0_kernel`:\n93\t\n94\t```cuda\n95\t__global__ void stacked_reduction_extract_s0_kernel(\n96\t    const FpExt *transform,    // [num_buckets × output_len × (3 * skip_domain)]\n97\t    const FpExt *g_buckets,    // [num_buckets × 3 * skip_domain]  \n98\t    FpExt *s0_out,             // [output_len]\n99\t    uint32_t num_buckets,\n100\t    uint32_t output_len,       // s_0_deg + 1\n101\t    uint32_t input_len         // 3 * skip_domain\n102\t) {\n103\t    // Each thread computes one output coefficient\n104\t    uint32_t out_idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n105\t    if (out_idx >= output_len) return;\n106\t    \n107\t    FpExt acc = FpExt::zero();\n108\t    for (uint32_t bucket = 0; bucket < num_buckets; bucket++) {\n109\t        const FpExt *T = transform + bucket * output_len * input_len;\n110\t        const FpExt *g = g_buckets + bucket * input_len;\n111\t        \n112\t        FpExt dot = FpExt::zero();\n113\t        for (uint32_t j = 0; j < input_len; j++) {\n114\t            dot += T[out_idx * input_len + j] * g[j];\n115\t        }\n116\t        acc += dot;\n117\t    }\n118\t    s0_out[out_idx] = acc;\n119\t}\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\tGrid: `(1, 1, 1)`, Block: `(output_len, 1, 1)` — just 5 threads for s_0_deg+1=5 coefficients. Each thread does 768 multiply-adds per bucket × 9 buckets ≈ 7000 multiply-adds. Total kernel time: ~10µs.\n123\t\n124\t**Note**: With only 5 threads, GPU utilization is very low. An alternative is to parallelize the inner dot product across threads using shared memory reduction. But given the tiny kernel time, this is unnecessary.\n125\t\n126\t### Step 4: Rust FFI wrapper\n127\t\n128\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n129\t\n130\tAdd:\n131\t```rust\n132\textern \"C\" {\n133\t    fn _stacked_reduction_extract_s0(\n134\t        transform: *const EF,\n135\t        g_buckets: *const EF,\n136\t        s0_out: *mut EF,\n137\t        num_buckets: u32,\n138\t        output_len: u32,\n139\t        input_len: u32,\n140\t    ) -> i32;\n141\t}\n142\t\n143\tpub unsafe fn stacked_reduction_extract_s0(\n144\t    d_transform: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n145\t    d_g_buckets: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n146\t    d_s0_out: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n147\t    num_buckets: u32,\n148\t    output_len: u32,\n149\t    input_len: u32,\n150\t) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n151\t    CudaError::from_result(_stacked_reduction_extract_s0(\n152\t        d_transform.as_ptr(),\n153\t        d_g_buckets.as_ptr(),\n154\t        d_s0_out.as_mut_ptr(),\n155\t        num_buckets,\n156\t        output_len,\n157\t        input_len,\n158\t    ))\n159\t}\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\t### Step 5: Replace reconstruct_s0_from_g\n163\t\n164\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n165\t\n166\tReplace the body of `reconstruct_s0_from_g` with:\n167\t\n168\t1. **CPU**: Compute E polynomials for each active bucket (unchanged from current code, ~100µs)\n169\t2. **CPU**: Build transformation matrices T for each active bucket:\n170\t   - For n≥0: T_pos from e0, e1, e2\n171\t   - For each n<0 bucket: T_neg_k from e0_neg, e1_neg, e2_neg\n172\t   - Total: ~1ms\n173\t3. **CPU→GPU**: Upload T matrices (concatenated flat buffer, ~500KB)\n174\t4. **GPU→GPU**: Concatenate d_g_pos and non-zero d_g_neg buffers into contiguous d_g_all\n175\t   - Or: just upload a pointer array and index into separate buffers\n176\t5. **GPU**: Launch `stacked_reduction_extract_s0_kernel`\n177\t6. **GPU→CPU**: `d_s0.to_host()` — only s_0_deg+1 = 5 values\n178\t\n179\t**Fallback**: If the G bucket is all-zero (checked via a device-side reduction or by tracking which buckets received contributions during the per-trace kernel loop), skip that bucket's T matrix computation entirely.\n180\t\n181\t### Step 6: Track active buckets\n182\t\n183\tDuring the per-trace kernel loop (lines 504-549), track which buckets received non-zero contributions by maintaining a bitmask `active_buckets: u16` (1 bit per bucket, including n≥0). This avoids the current per-bucket `g_neg.iter().all(|&x| x == EF::ZERO)` check which requires D2H.\n184\t\n185\t## Invariants\n186\t\n187\t1. **Correctness**: The output `s_0` polynomial must be identical to the current CPU-computed result. The transformation matrix approach is mathematically equivalent — it pre-computes the linear map that the sequential iDFT/DFT/multiply pipeline implements.\n188\t\n189\t2. **Bucket accumulation**: The per-trace GPU kernel accumulation into d_g_pos/d_g_neg must remain unchanged. Only the post-accumulation reconstruction changes.\n190\t\n191\t3. **No protocol change**: The sumcheck polynomial `s_0`, its degree, and the transcript interaction (observe s_0 evaluations, sample challenge) remain unchanged.\n192\t\n193\t4. **No APC 0 regression**: The computation is the same at APC 0 (same l_skip, same bucket structure). Expected improvement similar across all APC configs since the Stacked Reduction is APC-independent.\n194\t\n195\t## Measurement Plan\n196\t\n197\tRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n198\t- **Primary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1095ms)\n199\t- **Component metric**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~60ms)\n200\t- **Regression check**: APC 0 STARK excl trace (expected: no regression, slight improvement)\n201\t\n202\tVerify correctness: all segments produce valid proofs (the benchmark runner verifies proofs).\n203\t\n204\t## Rollback Criteria\n205\t\n206\tRevert if any of:\n207\t1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n208\t2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n209\t3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n210\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BV2cjEaR1zKGBoe6AzNm2L","session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"eadb7293-9e7e-4565-b80d-119aa28883a7","timestamp":"2026-04-16T05:23:32.731Z"}
