{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016EzzRkqotJqJ8fheLhTPtM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed: `s_0_deg = 2 * (256 - 1) = 510` when `l_skip = 8`. The output polynomial has 511 coefficients, not 5.\n\nThis changes everything. The dense transformation matrix approach is O(n²) while NTT is O(n log n). With n=512:\n- NTT: 512 × 9 ≈ 4600 operations\n- Dense matrix: 511 × 768 ≈ 392,000 operations (85× more)\n\n\nThe dense matrix approach won't work here. I'm switching to a GPU-side NTT pipeline using the existing device primitives, where I compute E coefficients on the CPU, upload them to the GPU, then perform inverse DFT on the G evaluation vectors, pad to 512, and run batched forward DFTs before pointwise multiplication and accumulation.\n\nNow I'm realizing the existing `batch_ntt` kernel might be optimized for much larger transforms, so the kernel launch overhead could become a bottleneck for these small 256-512 element operations. A fused kernel that handles the entire iDFT-DFT-multiply pipeline in shared memory would be significantly more efficient.\n\nWith 512 elements fitting comfortably in shared memory, I can design a single-block kernel that loads the skip_domain G evaluations, performs an in-place iDFT, pads to the larger output domain size, loads the E coefficients, performs DFTs on both vectors, and then executes the pointwise multiplication all within one kernel launch.\n\nThe kernel accumulates the products into an s vector, applies a final iDFT, and writes the s_0 coefficients back to global memory using atomic operations to handle cross-bucket contributions. With 256 threads per block handling 2 elements each for the 512-point NTT, the shared memory footprint of 44KB stays well under the 128KB limit, leaving room for efficient execution.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens"