{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019gFdD1y6kqfr9qhfZgr5c8","type":"tool_result","content":"110\t\n111\t// Decimation in time NTT: bit reversed order to natural order.\n112\t//\n113\t// Uses exactly `2^l_skip` threads, and `this_thread_value` will be set with the output of the NTT.\n114\t// For both `needs_shmem` equals `true` and `false`, `this_thread_value` must already contain the input, which should be the `rev_len(i, l_skip)`-th element.\n115\t//\n116\t// # Assumption\n117\t// - No warp has exited early before this call. We use the full `0xffffffff` mask.\n118\ttemplate <bool intt, bool needs_shmem>\n119\t__device__ __forceinline__ void ntt_bitrev_to_natural(\n120\t    Fp &this_thread_value,\n121\t    Fp *__restrict__ sbuf,\n122\t    uint32_t const i,\n123\t    uint32_t const l_skip\n124\t) {\n125\t    uint32_t const warp_levels = needs_shmem ? LOG_WARP_SIZE : l_skip;\n126\t\n127\t    // Warp shuffle phase: levels 0 to warp_levels-1 (small to large)\n128\t    for (uint32_t m = 0; m < warp_levels; m++) {\n129\t        uint32_t const len = 1u << m;\n130\t        // Twiddle for DIT: primitive 2^(m+1)-th root raised to (i & (len-1))\n131\t        uint32_t j = i & (len - 1);\n132\t        if constexpr (intt)\n133\t            j = j ? ((2u << m) - j) : 0;\n134\t        Fp twiddle = get_twiddle(m + 1, j);\n135\t\n136\t        Fp other = Fp::fromRaw(__shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, this_thread_value.asRaw(), len));\n137\t\n138\t        if (i & len) {\n139\t            // Bottom: result = a - b * twiddle\n140\t            Fp b_tw = this_thread_value * twiddle;\n141\t            this_thread_value = sum_or_semi_sum<intt>(other - b_tw);\n142\t        } else {\n143\t            // Top: result = a + b * twiddle\n144\t            Fp b_tw = other * twiddle;\n145\t            this_thread_value = sum_or_semi_sum<intt>(this_thread_value + b_tw);\n146\t        }\n147\t    }\n148\t\n149\t    // Shared memory phase: levels LOG_WARP_SIZE to l_skip-1\n150\t    if constexpr (needs_shmem) {\n151\t        sbuf[i] = this_thread_value;\n152\t        __syncthreads();\n153\t\n154\t        for (uint32_t m = LOG_WARP_SIZE; m < l_skip; m++) {\n155\t            uint32_t const len = 1u << m;\n156\t            uint32_t j = i & (len - 1);\n157\t            if constexpr (intt)\n158\t                j = j ? ((2u << m) - j) : 0;\n159\t            Fp twiddle = get_twiddle(m + 1, j);\n160\t\n161\t            if (!(i & len)) {\n162\t                Fp a = sbuf[i];\n163\t                Fp b_tw = sbuf[i + len] * twiddle;\n164\t                sbuf[i] = sum_or_semi_sum<intt>(a + b_tw);\n165\t                sbuf[i + len] = sum_or_semi_sum<intt>(a - b_tw);\n166\t            }\n167\t            __syncthreads();\n168\t        }\n169\t        this_thread_value = sbuf[i];\n170\t    }\n171\t}\n172\t\n173\t} // namespace device_ntt\n174\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TvpUjpCExtn5Q8Q5HMqHC9","session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"dc211a3f-9cf4-4fd5-81ff-fca1730f99a9","timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:03:11.619Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aa486eddedc4632d1","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TvpUjpCExtn5Q8Q5HMqHC9","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"Review revised plan (round 2)","usage":{"total_tokens":66826,"tool_uses":42,"duration_ms":189573},"uuid":"fcd7bffa-723e-42f0-980f-d7e6ea09bd1f","session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TvpUjpCExtn5Q8Q5HMqHC9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the information needed for a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1. NTT size mismatch: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` requires exactly `2^l_skip` threads, but kernel uses 512 threads for 256-element iDFT (DESIGN BLOCKER)\n\nThe plan proposes launching the kernel with `large_uni_domain = 512` threads per block. For the iDFT of G evaluations (skip_domain = 256 = 2^8), it calls `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true>` with `l_skip=8`. The documentation on `device_ntt.cuh` line 50 states: \"Uses exactly `2^l_skip` threads.\" The `__shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, ...)` calls at line 99 use a full 32-thread warp mask. When 512 threads exist in the block but only 256 participate in the l_skip=8 NTT, the shared memory phase (lines 80-93) has `__syncthreads()` which syncs ALL 512 threads, and the warp shuffle phase (lines 96-108) uses `0xffffffff` as the mask across all 32 threads in a warp.\n\nThe problem is: if thread 256-511 call `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` with `l_skip=8` and `i = threadIdx.x` (which is 256-511, exceeding the `[0, 2^l_skip)` range), this will read/write out-of-bounds into the shared memory buffer of size 256, and the twiddle accesses at `get_twiddle(8, idx)` where `idx >= 256` are undefined.\n\nThe plan sketches using `active_thread=false` for threads 256-511 during the 256-element iDFT. While `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` does have an `active_thread` parameter, there is a subtlety: the inactive threads still execute `this_thread_value = sbuf[i]` at line 93 and the warp shuffle at line 99 unconditionally (these are outside the `if (active_thread)` guard in the shared memory phase). With `i >= 256` and a shared buffer of size 256, `sbuf[i]` at line 93 is an out-of-bounds read. This is a design blocker.\n\nThe plan must either:\n- Use separate shared memory buffers and thread subgroups for the 256-element and 512-element NTTs (complex).\n- Run the 256-element iDFT, write the result, sync, then do a 512-element DFT with all threads (cleaner, but requires careful staging).\n\n### F2. Bit-reversal ordering mismatch between iDFT output and DFT input (DESIGN BLOCKER)\n\n`ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true>` takes natural-order input and produces bit-reversed output. `ntt_bitrev_to_natural<intt=false>` takes bit-reversed input and produces natural-order output. The plan's kernel pseudocode (Step 2 lines 134-141) chains: iDFT (natural to bitrev) on G, then pads to large_uni_domain, then DFT (bitrev to natural) on the padded G.\n\nThe problem: after the 256-element iDFT, the coefficients are in **bit-reversed** order in the 256-element shared buffer. To pad to 512 and then DFT on 512 elements, the plan implies the coefficients sit in positions 0..255 and zeros fill 256..511. But the coefficients are bit-reversed over 8 bits, not in natural order. Padding zeros after bit-reversed data does not produce a valid zero-padded coefficient array in either natural or bit-reversed order for a 512-element NTT.\n\nThe correct approach: after the 256-element iNTT (natural-to-bitrev), either (a) bit-reverse back to natural order, zero-pad to 512, then bit-reverse for the 512-element DIT, or (b) use batch_ntt_small which handles the bit-reversal permutation in global memory (`buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = this_thread_value`). Either way, this requires explicit bit-reversal between the two NTT stages, which the plan does not specify.\n\n### F3. No existing `atomicAddExt` for FpExt -- the plan references a non-existent primitive\n\nThe plan's kernel pseudocode at line 165 calls `atomicAddExt(&s0_out[tid], s_smem[tid])`. Searching the entire CUDA codebase confirms there is no `atomicAddExt` function. FpExt is 16 bytes (4 x Fp), and CUDA only provides native `atomicAdd` for 32-bit and 64-bit types. An FpExt atomic add would require 4 separate `atomicAdd` calls on the individual `Fp` components, each of which operates modulo the BabyBear prime. Since `Fp` uses a Montgomery representation (via `bb31_t`), a raw `atomicAdd` on the uint32 representation is not a valid field addition.\n\nThis must be implemented as 4 separate atomic CAS loops or, more practically, as a two-pass approach: each block writes to separate storage, then a reduction kernel sums across buckets.\n\n### F4. Shared memory usage underestimated\n\nThe plan states \"4 x large_uni_domain x sizeof(FpExt) = 4 x 512 x 16 = 32KB.\" Breaking down the actual needs:\n- G work buffer: 3 x 512 x 16 = 24,576 bytes (for 3 G vectors in shared mem)\n- s_smem result buffer: 512 x 16 = 8,192 bytes\n- Fp temp buffer for NTT per-component extraction: 512 x 4 = 2,048 bytes\n- Total: ~34,816 bytes (34KB, as the plan later corrects)\n\nBut additionally, `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<needs_shmem=true>` expects its own `Fp sbuf` of size `2^l_skip`. When running on the Fp components sequentially, the 512-Fp buffer (2KB) overlaps with the temp buffer mentioned above. The total is indeed around 34KB which is within the 128KB limit. However, the plan does not account for the fact that extracting Fp components from FpExt in shared memory, running NTT, and writing back requires careful aliasing. The G vectors remain in shared memory as FpExt while one component is being NTT'd in a separate Fp buffer. This means the FpExt shared memory and Fp NTT shared memory cannot overlap. The 34KB figure is approximately correct.\n\n### F5. The claimed benefit of ~10-15ms is optimistic for 9 blocks x 512 threads\n\nThe plan claims the CPU reconstruction overhead is ~16-24ms across 2 segments and the GPU kernel will eliminate most of it. But consider what the GPU kernel actually does: 9 blocks (one per bucket), each processing 10 NTT operations on 256-512 Fp elements sequentially (4 components x 10 NTT operations = 40 Fp NTTs per bucket). With 512 threads per block and 9 blocks, this is only 4,608 threads total -- a tiny fraction of GPU occupancy. The NTT operations are sequential within each block (each operates on the same shared memory). The GPU will likely be almost entirely idle.\n\nAt ~1 microsecond per small in-shared-memory NTT (256-512 Fp elements), 40 NTTs per bucket x 9 buckets = 360 NTTs. Even with zero overhead, the kernel itself might take ~0.4ms. The real savings would come from eliminating D2H copies and stream syncs. The plan estimates 18 syncs x 200us = 3.6ms from syncs alone, and an additional ~6-8ms per segment from CPU NTT work. This seems roughly plausible if there are indeed 9 syncing D2H copies per segment.\n\nHowever, the plan adds NEW CPU work in Step 5: pre-DFT of E coefficients. It estimates ~2.5ms for this, plus the poly_multiply_ntt for n<0 buckets is unchanged. The net wall-clock savings may be smaller than claimed if the E pre-DFT overlaps poorly with the GPU kernel launch.\n\n### F6. Expected improvement of ~10-15ms on a 74ms span is modest\n\nThe plan targets 74ms Stacked Reduction, expecting to reduce it to 60-65ms (save ~10-14ms). At APC 300, STARK excl trace is 1110ms, so this is roughly a 1% improvement on the primary metric. The rollback criterion of \"5ms improvement\" is appropriately conservative, but this should be evaluated against the implementation complexity (new fused CUDA kernel, FFI bridge, Fp component extraction logic).\n\n### F7. Active bucket detection changes are underspecified\n\nThe plan proposes tracking active n values via a `BTreeSet<i8>` during the per-trace kernel loop (Step 4). This is correct in principle -- the current code at lines 504-549 already computes `n = log_height as isize - l_skip as isize` per trace and selects the output bucket. Adding a set to track which buckets receive data is straightforward. However, the plan does not specify: (a) whether ALL l_skip d_g_neg buffers still need to be allocated even if inactive (currently they all are at line 496), (b) how the active bucket indices map to the contiguous g_buckets array passed to the GPU kernel, and (c) how the pointer-array or contiguous buffer for g_buckets is constructed from the scattered d_g_pos/d_g_neg DeviceBuffers.\n\n### F8. Fallback path for l_skip > 10 not implemented\n\nThe plan mentions in Invariant 3 that l_skip > 10 should fall back to CPU, and in rollback criteria point 4. However, the implementation checklist does not include adding this guard. The current code does not check for l_skip > 10 in `reconstruct_s0_from_g`. If l_skip is always 8 in practice, this is a cleanup issue, not a blocker.\n\n---\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### I1. Use batch_ntt_small directly instead of a fused kernel\n\nRather than writing a complex fused kernel that manually extracts Fp components and handles two different NTT sizes in shared memory, consider:\n1. Keep G data on GPU (no D2H).\n2. Run existing `batch_ntt_small` for the iDFT of G (reinterpreting EF buffer as Fp with appropriate AoS->SoA transpose).\n3. Zero-pad on GPU via a simple kernel.\n4. Run `batch_ntt_small` again for the forward DFT of both E and G.\n5. Pointwise multiply+accumulate via a simple kernel.\n6. Run `batch_ntt_small` for the final iDFT.\n7. Single D2H at the end.\n\nThis avoids all the shared memory complexity, bit-reversal issues, and atomicAdd problems. The cost is more kernel launches (~5-6 instead of 1) and AoS/SoA transpose kernels, but the kernel launches are cheap (<10us each) and the approach is much easier to get correct. The main win (eliminating D2H syncs) is preserved.\n\n### I2. Overlap CPU E computation with GPU G iDFT\n\nIf using the multi-kernel approach above, the CPU computation of E polynomials can overlap with the GPU iDFT of G, since they are independent. The CPU work (eq_uni_poly, indicator polynomial multiplication) takes ~1-2ms; the GPU iDFT takes ~0.1ms. Use async copies and event-based synchronization to overlap.\n\n### I3. Keep the zero-check on GPU instead of tracking n values\n\nInstead of maintaining a BTreeSet on the CPU side, upload all 9 buffers to the GPU kernel and have each block check its own buffer for all-zeros in shared memory (a simple parallel reduction). This is simpler than the tracking approach and avoids potential bugs from n-value tracking.\n\n---\n\n## Assessment\n\n**There are two design-level blockers**: (F1) the 512-thread kernel calling `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` with l_skip=8 causes out-of-bounds shared memory access for threads 256-511, and (F2) the bit-reversal ordering mismatch between the 256-element iNTT output and the 512-element DFT input means the zero-padding step would produce incorrect results. Additionally (F3), the `atomicAddExt` primitive does not exist and a correct field-element atomic add requires non-trivial implementation.\n\n**The plan is not prototype-ready** as written. The fused kernel approach has three correctness holes that each require rethinking the kernel's thread/shared-memory strategy.\n\n**The strongest part of the plan** is the analysis of the current bottleneck: the per-bucket D2H + stream sync pattern is correctly identified as the primary waste, the NTT decomposition of EF into 4 independent Fp NTTs is mathematically correct, and the measurement that ~16ms of the 74ms Stacked Reduction time is non-kernel overhead is consistent with the cited profiling data. The alternative of using `batch_ntt_small` in a multi-kernel pipeline would preserve these correct insights while avoiding the kernel-level complexity that introduces the blockers."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa486eddedc4632d1 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa486eddedc4632d1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 69346\ntool_uses: 42\nduration_ms: 189574</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"0dd464d0-968d-49b0-9984-bc0212303c39","timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:04:35.543Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are reviewing an implementation plan for a GPU STARK prover optimization. This is the SECOND review iteration — the first review found a critical bug (s_0 degree was 510, not 4) which invalidated the original dense transformation matrix approach. The plan has been revised to use a fused GPU NTT kernel instead.\n\nReview this plan following the review guideline. Return your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.\n\n## Files to read (in order):\n1. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/review_guideline.md — the review standard\n2. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/context.md — project context\n3. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md — the task being planned\n4. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction/plan.md — the REVISED plan to review\n\n## Additional context — you MUST read these source files to verify claims:\n- /home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs (lines 481-686) — current CPU reconstruction\n- /home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/device_ntt.cuh — the device NTT primitives referenced in the plan\n- /home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu (first 200 lines) — existing CUDA kernels for stacked reduction\n- /home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/batch_ntt_small.rs — batch NTT wrapper\n\nKey facts for validation:\n- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1110ms, Stacked Reduction: 74ms\n- GPU kernel time within Stacked Reduction: ~58ms (block_sum 22ms + fold_ple 18ms + batch_fold_mle 11ms + w_moments 7ms)\n- Non-kernel overhead in Stacked Reduction: ~16ms\n- s_0_deg = 2 * (2^l_skip - 1) = 510 when l_skip=8\n- device_ntt supports NTT up to size 2^10 = 1024 (MAX_NTT_LEVEL=10)\n- Extension field EF is BabyBear4 (4 BabyBear components)\n\nCritical review questions:\n1. Is the NTT decomposition of EF into 4 independent Fp NTTs correct for base-field twiddles?\n2. Is the shared memory estimate correct? (4 × large_uni_domain × sizeof(FpExt) = 4 × 512 × 16 = 32KB — but the plan mentions processing Fp components sequentially, which changes memory needs)\n3. Are the 10 NTT operations per bucket realistic, and does the GPU kernel actually save time vs CPU?\n4. Does the `device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev` work correctly when called with l_skip=9 (large_uni_domain=512) using 512 threads?\n5. Is the active bucket detection via tracking n values correct?\n6. Is the Pre-DFT E approach (Step 5) adding CPU work that offsets the savings?\n\nReturn: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.","agentId":"aa486eddedc4632d1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the information needed for a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1. NTT size mismatch: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` requires exactly `2^l_skip` threads, but kernel uses 512 threads for 256-element iDFT (DESIGN BLOCKER)\n\nThe plan proposes launching the kernel with `large_uni_domain = 512` threads per block. For the iDFT of G evaluations (skip_domain = 256 = 2^8), it calls `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true>` with `l_skip=8`. The documentation on `device_ntt.cuh` line 50 states: \"Uses exactly `2^l_skip` threads.\" The `__shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, ...)` calls at line 99 use a full 32-thread warp mask. When 512 threads exist in the block but only 256 participate in the l_skip=8 NTT, the shared memory phase (lines 80-93) has `__syncthreads()` which syncs ALL 512 threads, and the warp shuffle phase (lines 96-108) uses `0xffffffff` as the mask across all 32 threads in a warp.\n\nThe problem is: if thread 256-511 call `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` with `l_skip=8` and `i = threadIdx.x` (which is 256-511, exceeding the `[0, 2^l_skip)` range), this will read/write out-of-bounds into the shared memory buffer of size 256, and the twiddle accesses at `get_twiddle(8, idx)` where `idx >= 256` are undefined.\n\nThe plan sketches using `active_thread=false` for threads 256-511 during the 256-element iDFT. While `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` does have an `active_thread` parameter, there is a subtlety: the inactive threads still execute `this_thread_value = sbuf[i]` at line 93 and the warp shuffle at line 99 unconditionally (these are outside the `if (active_thread)` guard in the shared memory phase). With `i >= 256` and a shared buffer of size 256, `sbuf[i]` at line 93 is an out-of-bounds read. This is a design blocker.\n\nThe plan must either:\n- Use separate shared memory buffers and thread subgroups for the 256-element and 512-element NTTs (complex).\n- Run the 256-element iDFT, write the result, sync, then do a 512-element DFT with all threads (cleaner, but requires careful staging).\n\n### F2. Bit-reversal ordering mismatch between iDFT output and DFT input (DESIGN BLOCKER)\n\n`ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true>` takes natural-order input and produces bit-reversed output. `ntt_bitrev_to_natural<intt=false>` takes bit-reversed input and produces natural-order output. The plan's kernel pseudocode (Step 2 lines 134-141) chains: iDFT (natural to bitrev) on G, then pads to large_uni_domain, then DFT (bitrev to natural) on the padded G.\n\nThe problem: after the 256-element iDFT, the coefficients are in **bit-reversed** order in the 256-element shared buffer. To pad to 512 and then DFT on 512 elements, the plan implies the coefficients sit in positions 0..255 and zeros fill 256..511. But the coefficients are bit-reversed over 8 bits, not in natural order. Padding zeros after bit-reversed data does not produce a valid zero-padded coefficient array in either natural or bit-reversed order for a 512-element NTT.\n\nThe correct approach: after the 256-element iNTT (natural-to-bitrev), either (a) bit-reverse back to natural order, zero-pad to 512, then bit-reverse for the 512-element DIT, or (b) use batch_ntt_small which handles the bit-reversal permutation in global memory (`buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = this_thread_value`). Either way, this requires explicit bit-reversal between the two NTT stages, which the plan does not specify.\n\n### F3. No existing `atomicAddExt` for FpExt -- the plan references a non-existent primitive\n\nThe plan's kernel pseudocode at line 165 calls `atomicAddExt(&s0_out[tid], s_smem[tid])`. Searching the entire CUDA codebase confirms there is no `atomicAddExt` function. FpExt is 16 bytes (4 x Fp), and CUDA only provides native `atomicAdd` for 32-bit and 64-bit types. An FpExt atomic add would require 4 separate `atomicAdd` calls on the individual `Fp` components, each of which operates modulo the BabyBear prime. Since `Fp` uses a Montgomery representation (via `bb31_t`), a raw `atomicAdd` on the uint32 representation is not a valid field addition.\n\nThis must be implemented as 4 separate atomic CAS loops or, more practically, as a two-pass approach: each block writes to separate storage, then a reduction kernel sums across buckets.\n\n### F4. Shared memory usage underestimated\n\nThe plan states \"4 x large_uni_domain x sizeof(FpExt) = 4 x 512 x 16 = 32KB.\" Breaking down the actual needs:\n- G work buffer: 3 x 512 x 16 = 24,576 bytes (for 3 G vectors in shared mem)\n- s_smem result buffer: 512 x 16 = 8,192 bytes\n- Fp temp buffer for NTT per-component extraction: 512 x 4 = 2,048 bytes\n- Total: ~34,816 bytes (34KB, as the plan later corrects)\n\nBut additionally, `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<needs_shmem=true>` expects its own `Fp sbuf` of size `2^l_skip`. When running on the Fp components sequentially, the 512-Fp buffer (2KB) overlaps with the temp buffer mentioned above. The total is indeed around 34KB which is within the 128KB limit. However, the plan does not account for the fact that extracting Fp components from FpExt in shared memory, running NTT, and writing back requires careful aliasing. The G vectors remain in shared memory as FpExt while one component is being NTT'd in a separate Fp buffer. This means the FpExt shared memory and Fp NTT shared memory cannot overlap. The 34KB figure is approximately correct.\n\n### F5. The claimed benefit of ~10-15ms is optimistic for 9 blocks x 512 threads\n\nThe plan claims the CPU reconstruction overhead is ~16-24ms across 2 segments and the GPU kernel will eliminate most of it. But consider what the GPU kernel actually does: 9 blocks (one per bucket), each processing 10 NTT operations on 256-512 Fp elements sequentially (4 components x 10 NTT operations = 40 Fp NTTs per bucket). With 512 threads per block and 9 blocks, this is only 4,608 threads total -- a tiny fraction of GPU occupancy. The NTT operations are sequential within each block (each operates on the same shared memory). The GPU will likely be almost entirely idle.\n\nAt ~1 microsecond per small in-shared-memory NTT (256-512 Fp elements), 40 NTTs per bucket x 9 buckets = 360 NTTs. Even with zero overhead, the kernel itself might take ~0.4ms. The real savings would come from eliminating D2H copies and stream syncs. The plan estimates 18 syncs x 200us = 3.6ms from syncs alone, and an additional ~6-8ms per segment from CPU NTT work. This seems roughly plausible if there are indeed 9 syncing D2H copies per segment.\n\nHowever, the plan adds NEW CPU work in Step 5: pre-DFT of E coefficients. It estimates ~2.5ms for this, plus the poly_multiply_ntt for n<0 buckets is unchanged. The net wall-clock savings may be smaller than claimed if the E pre-DFT overlaps poorly with the GPU kernel launch.\n\n### F6. Expected improvement of ~10-15ms on a 74ms span is modest\n\nThe plan targets 74ms Stacked Reduction, expecting to reduce it to 60-65ms (save ~10-14ms). At APC 300, STARK excl trace is 1110ms, so this is roughly a 1% improvement on the primary metric. The rollback criterion of \"5ms improvement\" is appropriately conservative, but this should be evaluated against the implementation complexity (new fused CUDA kernel, FFI bridge, Fp component extraction logic).\n\n### F7. Active bucket detection changes are underspecified\n\nThe plan proposes tracking active n values via a `BTreeSet<i8>` during the per-trace kernel loop (Step 4). This is correct in principle -- the current code at lines 504-549 already computes `n = log_height as isize - l_skip as isize` per trace and selects the output bucket. Adding a set to track which buckets receive data is straightforward. However, the plan does not specify: (a) whether ALL l_skip d_g_neg buffers still need to be allocated even if inactive (currently they all are at line 496), (b) how the active bucket indices map to the contiguous g_buckets array passed to the GPU kernel, and (c) how the pointer-array or contiguous buffer for g_buckets is constructed from the scattered d_g_pos/d_g_neg DeviceBuffers.\n\n### F8. Fallback path for l_skip > 10 not implemented\n\nThe plan mentions in Invariant 3 that l_skip > 10 should fall back to CPU, and in rollback criteria point 4. However, the implementation checklist does not include adding this guard. The current code does not check for l_skip > 10 in `reconstruct_s0_from_g`. If l_skip is always 8 in practice, this is a cleanup issue, not a blocker.\n\n---\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### I1. Use batch_ntt_small directly instead of a fused kernel\n\nRather than writing a complex fused kernel that manually extracts Fp components and handles two different NTT sizes in shared memory, consider:\n1. Keep G data on GPU (no D2H).\n2. Run existing `batch_ntt_small` for the iDFT of G (reinterpreting EF buffer as Fp with appropriate AoS->SoA transpose).\n3. Zero-pad on GPU via a simple kernel.\n4. Run `batch_ntt_small` again for the forward DFT of both E and G.\n5. Pointwise multiply+accumulate via a simple kernel.\n6. Run `batch_ntt_small` for the final iDFT.\n7. Single D2H at the end.\n\nThis avoids all the shared memory complexity, bit-reversal issues, and atomicAdd problems. The cost is more kernel launches (~5-6 instead of 1) and AoS/SoA transpose kernels, but the kernel launches are cheap (<10us each) and the approach is much easier to get correct. The main win (eliminating D2H syncs) is preserved.\n\n### I2. Overlap CPU E computation with GPU G iDFT\n\nIf using the multi-kernel approach above, the CPU computation of E polynomials can overlap with the GPU iDFT of G, since they are independent. The CPU work (eq_uni_poly, indicator polynomial multiplication) takes ~1-2ms; the GPU iDFT takes ~0.1ms. Use async copies and event-based synchronization to overlap.\n\n### I3. Keep the zero-check on GPU instead of tracking n values\n\nInstead of maintaining a BTreeSet on the CPU side, upload all 9 buffers to the GPU kernel and have each block check its own buffer for all-zeros in shared memory (a simple parallel reduction). This is simpler than the tracking approach and avoids potential bugs from n-value tracking.\n\n---\n\n## Assessment\n\n**There are two design-level blockers**: (F1) the 512-thread kernel calling `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` with l_skip=8 causes out-of-bounds shared memory access for threads 256-511, and (F2) the bit-reversal ordering mismatch between the 256-element iNTT output and the 512-element DFT input means the zero-padding step would produce incorrect results. Additionally (F3), the `atomicAddExt` primitive does not exist and a correct field-element atomic add requires non-trivial implementation.\n\n**The plan is not prototype-ready** as written. The fused kernel approach has three correctness holes that each require rethinking the kernel's thread/shared-memory strategy.\n\n**The strongest part of the plan** is the analysis of the current bottleneck: the per-bucket D2H + stream sync pattern is correctly identified as the primary waste, the NTT decomposition of EF into 4 independent Fp NTTs is mathematically correct, and the measurement that ~16ms of the 74ms Stacked Reduction time is non-kernel overhead is consistent with the cited profiling data. The alternative of using `batch_ntt_small` in a multi-kernel pipeline would preserve these correct insights while avoiding the kernel-level complexity that introduces the blockers."}],"totalDurationMs":189574,"totalTokens":69346,"totalToolUseCount":42,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1230,"cache_read_input_tokens":64823,"output_tokens":3292,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1230},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3292,"cache_read_input_tokens":64823,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1230,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":17,"searchCount":22,"bashCount":3,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XYLykfcYg9JHerATqiPoUp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The second review found 3 design-level blockers in the fused kernel approach:\n1. F1: Thread count mismatch for 256-element iDFT with 512 threads\n2. F2: Bit-reversal ordering mismatch between iNTT output and DFT input\n3. F3: Non-existent atomicAddExt for FpExt\n\nThe reviewer strongly recommends alternative I1: use batch_ntt_small directly instead of a fused kernel. This is a multi-kernel pipeline that:\n1. Keep G on GPU\n2. AoS→SoA transpose\n\n\n3. batch_ntt_small for iDFT of G\n4. Zero-pad\n5. batch_ntt_small for DFT of E and G\n6. Pointwise multiply + accumulate\n7. batch_ntt_small for final iDFT\n8. Single D2H\n\nThis avoids all the shared memory complexity and is much simpler to implement correctly. I'm going to drop the fused kernel entirely and instead build out the pipeline with batch_ntt_small, writing a small transpose kernel for AoS→SoA conversion and a pointwise multiply+accumulate kernel to handle the computation.\n\nFor the output, I'll use separate per-bucket buffers to avoid atomics, then sum across buckets with a simple reduction kernel to finalize the results.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4313,"cache_read_input_tokens":233624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4313},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"8bbe399c-b7ef-448c-b141-622f28b775b1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XYLykfcYg9JHerATqiPoUp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good review — 3 design blockers in the fused kernel. The reviewer recommends using `batch_ntt_small` in a multi-kernel pipeline instead. Let me revise the plan to address all blockers."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4313,"cache_read_input_tokens":233624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4313},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"3cc72051-cda1-4c8b-87ae-57b73b537dbd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XYLykfcYg9JHerATqiPoUp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P9q3AEhWjaRgatriYoaRcD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction/plan.md","content":"# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n\n## Goal\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n\nPer-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:\n- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n\nThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:\n1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)\n2. Check all-zero, skip if so\n3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU\n4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline\n\n### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)\n- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512\n- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients\n- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements\n- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)\n\n### Why it's slow\n- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**\n- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**\n\n## Changes\n\n### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline\n\nReplace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity.\n\nPipeline per bucket:\n1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)\n2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)\n3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients\n4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain\n5. CPU: Compute E coefficients, DFT to evaluation domain, upload as SoA Fp\n6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals\n7. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n8. GPU: AoS → SoA of s (if needed for iDFT)\n9. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients\n10. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add)\n\nFinal: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB).\n\n### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA transpose kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a small CUDA kernel to convert between AoS (FpExt layout) and SoA (4 × Fp layout):\n\n```cuda\n// AoS to SoA: input[n] = (c0,c1,c2,c3) → output[comp*n + i] for each comp\n__global__ void ef_aos_to_soa(\n    const uint32_t *input,   // n * 4 uint32_t (FpExt as 4 × Fp)\n    uint32_t *output,        // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA: all c0, then all c1, ...)\n    uint32_t n\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= n) return;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        output[c * n + i] = input[i * 4 + c];\n    }\n}\n\n// SoA to AoS: reverse of above\n__global__ void ef_soa_to_aos(\n    const uint32_t *input,   // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA)\n    uint32_t *output,        // n * 4 uint32_t (AoS, FpExt)\n    uint32_t n\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= n) return;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        output[i * 4 + c] = input[c * n + i];\n    }\n}\n```\n\nGrid: `(ceil(n/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For n=768 (3 × skip_domain): 3 blocks. Trivial.\n\nAdd C launchers and Rust FFI wrappers in the corresponding files.\n\n### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\n```cuda\n// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)\n// Output s is AoS FpExt format\n__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(\n    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]\n    uint32_t domain_size\n) {\n    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (j >= domain_size) return;\n    FpExt acc = FpExt::zero();\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];\n    }\n    s_out[j] = acc;\n}\n```\n\nGrid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks.\n\n### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel\n\n```cuda\n// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len\n__global__ void ef_accumulate(\n    FpExt *accum,\n    const FpExt *addend,\n    uint32_t len\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= len) return;\n    accum[i] += addend[i];\n}\n```\n\nNo atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream.\n\n### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:\n- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`\n- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`\n- `ef_accumulate`\n\n### Step 5: GPU pipeline function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:\n\n```rust\nfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device\n    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)\n    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip\n    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain\n    l_skip: usize,\n    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements\n    // Pre-allocated work buffers:\n    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements\n    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements\n) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n```\n\nImplementation:\n1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements\n\n2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`\n   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements\n   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order\n\n3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block.\n\n4. **Bit-reverse + zero-pad**: Copy d_g_aos_padded into the first skip_domain positions of a 3 × large_uni_domain buffer. The bit-reversal from step 2 needs to be undone. **Since batch_ntt_small handles bit-reversal internally** (the kernel writes `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = result`), the output IS in natural order. So we just copy and zero-pad.\n\n   Actually, let me clarify. Looking at `batch_ntt_small.cu` — the kernel `batch_ntt_small_kernel` at the end writes: `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. So the output is bit-reversed per block. We need to check whether `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order or bit-reversed output.\n\n   Reading the kernel source: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<INTT>` produces bit-reversed output. Then the kernel writes `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. Since `rev_len` reverses the bit pattern and `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` produces bit-reversed output, applying `rev_len` to the index gives natural-order output in global memory. **So the output of batch_ntt_small is in natural order.**\n\n   → Zero-pad: copy 3 × skip_domain coefficients into positions 0..skip_domain of each 3 × large_uni_domain segment, zeros in skip_domain..large_uni_domain.\n\n5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`\n\n6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)\n\n7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E\n\n8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply\n\n9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`\n\n10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT\n\n11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`\n\n12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt\n\n13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`\n\n### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nReplace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n\n1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.\n\n2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):\n   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each\n   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)\n\n3. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):\n   a. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients (eq_uni_poly, indicator multiply) — unchanged from current\n   b. **CPU**: Pad E coefficients to large_uni_domain, arrange in flat [3 × large_uni_domain] EF array\n   c. **CPU→GPU**: Upload E coefficients as EF via `to_device()`\n   d. **GPU**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, e_coeffs, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`\n\n4. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements\n5. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n\n### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL\n\nAt the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness.\n\n2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation.\n\n3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering).\n\n4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n\n5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)\n- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)\n- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)\n\nVerify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if any of:\n1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4313,"cache_read_input_tokens":233624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4313},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"6ac53a92-d7ea-407f-8d6e-11307171267a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P9q3AEhWjaRgatriYoaRcD","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"98830126-662e-4e5e-aa9c-fee6bae4a25a","timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:06:11.396Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction/plan.md","content":"# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n\n## Goal\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n\nPer-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:\n- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n\nThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:\n1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)\n2. Check all-zero, skip if so\n3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU\n4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline\n\n### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)\n- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512\n- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients\n- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements\n- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)\n\n### Why it's slow\n- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**\n- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**\n\n## Changes\n\n### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline\n\nReplace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity.\n\nPipeline per bucket:\n1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)\n2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)\n3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients\n4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain\n5. CPU: Compute E coefficients, DFT to evaluation domain, upload as SoA Fp\n6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals\n7. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n8. GPU: AoS → SoA of s (if needed for iDFT)\n9. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients\n10. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add)\n\nFinal: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB).\n\n### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA transpose kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a small CUDA kernel to convert between AoS (FpExt layout) and SoA (4 × Fp layout):\n\n```cuda\n// AoS to SoA: input[n] = (c0,c1,c2,c3) → output[comp*n + i] for each comp\n__global__ void ef_aos_to_soa(\n    const uint32_t *input,   // n * 4 uint32_t (FpExt as 4 × Fp)\n    uint32_t *output,        // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA: all c0, then all c1, ...)\n    uint32_t n\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= n) return;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        output[c * n + i] = input[i * 4 + c];\n    }\n}\n\n// SoA to AoS: reverse of above\n__global__ void ef_soa_to_aos(\n    const uint32_t *input,   // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA)\n    uint32_t *output,        // n * 4 uint32_t (AoS, FpExt)\n    uint32_t n\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= n) return;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        output[i * 4 + c] = input[c * n + i];\n    }\n}\n```\n\nGrid: `(ceil(n/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For n=768 (3 × skip_domain): 3 blocks. Trivial.\n\nAdd C launchers and Rust FFI wrappers in the corresponding files.\n\n### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\n```cuda\n// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)\n// Output s is AoS FpExt format\n__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(\n    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]\n    uint32_t domain_size\n) {\n    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (j >= domain_size) return;\n    FpExt acc = FpExt::zero();\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];\n    }\n    s_out[j] = acc;\n}\n```\n\nGrid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks.\n\n### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel\n\n```cuda\n// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len\n__global__ void ef_accumulate(\n    FpExt *accum,\n    const FpExt *addend,\n    uint32_t len\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= len) return;\n    accum[i] += addend[i];\n}\n```\n\nNo atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream.\n\n### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:\n- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`\n- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`\n- `ef_accumulate`\n\n### Step 5: GPU pipeline function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:\n\n```rust\nfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device\n    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)\n    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip\n    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain\n    l_skip: usize,\n    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements\n    // Pre-allocated work buffers:\n    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements\n    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements\n) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n```\n\nImplementation:\n1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements\n\n2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`\n   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements\n   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order\n\n3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block.\n\n4. **Bit-reverse + zero-pad**: Copy d_g_aos_padded into the first skip_domain positions of a 3 × large_uni_domain buffer. The bit-reversal from step 2 needs to be undone. **Since batch_ntt_small handles bit-reversal internally** (the kernel writes `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = result`), the output IS in natural order. So we just copy and zero-pad.\n\n   Actually, let me clarify. Looking at `batch_ntt_small.cu` — the kernel `batch_ntt_small_kernel` at the end writes: `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. So the output is bit-reversed per block. We need to check whether `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order or bit-reversed output.\n\n   Reading the kernel source: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<INTT>` produces bit-reversed output. Then the kernel writes `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. Since `rev_len` reverses the bit pattern and `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` produces bit-reversed output, applying `rev_len` to the index gives natural-order output in global memory. **So the output of batch_ntt_small is in natural order.**\n\n   → Zero-pad: copy 3 × skip_domain coefficients into positions 0..skip_domain of each 3 × large_uni_domain segment, zeros in skip_domain..large_uni_domain.\n\n5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`\n\n6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)\n\n7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E\n\n8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply\n\n9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`\n\n10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT\n\n11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`\n\n12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt\n\n13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`\n\n### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nReplace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n\n1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.\n\n2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):\n   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each\n   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)\n\n3. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):\n   a. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients (eq_uni_poly, indicator multiply) — unchanged from current\n   b. **CPU**: Pad E coefficients to large_uni_domain, arrange in flat [3 × large_uni_domain] EF array\n   c. **CPU→GPU**: Upload E coefficients as EF via `to_device()`\n   d. **GPU**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, e_coeffs, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`\n\n4. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements\n5. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n\n### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL\n\nAt the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness.\n\n2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation.\n\n3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering).\n\n4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n\n5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)\n- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)\n- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)\n\nVerify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if any of:\n1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":254,"newStart":2,"newLines":256,"lines":[" "," ## Goal"," ","-Move the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using batched device NTT primitives. The current CPU pipeline performs per-bucket D2H transfers (each syncing the stream), followed by serial iDFT / DFT / pointwise-multiply / iDFT operations. Replacing this with GPU-side NTT eliminates ~8 redundant stream syncs and moves the NTT compute to GPU where it can run in parallel.","+Move the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span."," "," ## Current Code Path"," "," ### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)"," ","-1. **Per-trace GPU kernel loop** (lines 504-549): For each trace, calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` which launches the `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` and `final_reduce_block_sums<true>`. Results accumulate (+=) into per-bucket GPU buffers:","-   - `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)","-   - `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)","+Per-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:","+- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)","+- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)"," ","-2. **CPU reconstruction** (line 552): Calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641)","-","-### `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (stacked_reduction.rs:566-641)","-","-**Key dimensions** (with l_skip=8):","-- skip_domain = 256","-- s_0_deg = `sumcheck_round0_deg(8, 2)` = 2 × (256 − 1) = **510**","-- large_uni_domain = 512 (next_power_of_two(511))","-- Output: 511 EF coefficients","-","-For each bucket (n≥0 and each active n<0):","-1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — **D2H + stream sync** (~200µs each)","-2. Check if all-zero (skip if so)","-3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU (eq_uni_poly, etc.)","+Then calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:","+1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)","+2. Check all-zero, skip if so","+3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU"," 4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`","-5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add`","+5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline"," ","-### `ntt_multiply_and_add` (stacked_reduction.rs:645-686)","+### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)","+- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512","+- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients","+- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements","+- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)"," ","-Per call (per bucket):","-1. **iDFT** of 3 G evaluation vectors (each 256 EF elements) → G coefficient vectors","-2. **Pad** both E and G coefficient vectors to 512 elements, transpose to column-major (512 × 3)","-3. **Batch DFT** of E and G matrices (3 columns × 512 rows each) → evaluation domain","-4. **Pointwise multiply**: s_eval[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j] (EF multiply at each of 512 points)","-5. **iDFT** of 512-element result → product coefficients","-6. **Accumulate** into s_0_coeffs","-"," ### Why it's slow","-","-Total across both APC 300 segments:"," - 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs","-- Per-bucket NTT work: ~8-10 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements per bucket × ~9 active buckets per segment","-- CPU NTT of 512 EF elements: each involves 4 independent BabyBear NTTs (from extension field decomposition). Each 512-element NTT ≈ 512 × 9 × 20ns = 92µs. With ~60-80 NTTs total per segment: **~6-8ms** per segment","-- Total CPU reconstruction overhead per segment: **~8-12ms**","-- Total across 2 segments: **~16-24ms**","+- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**","+- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**"," ","-Non-kernel overhead in Stacked Reduction: 74ms − 58ms (GPU kernels) = **~16ms**, consistent with the estimate above.","-"," ## Changes"," ","-### Step 1: Add GPU NTT pipeline function for polynomial product-sum","+### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`","+Replace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity."," ","-Add a new function `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add` that replaces the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` by doing the NTT pipeline on GPU:","+Pipeline per bucket:","+1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)","+2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)","+3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients","+4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain","+5. CPU: Compute E coefficients, DFT to evaluation domain, upload as SoA Fp","+6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals","+7. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]","+8. GPU: AoS → SoA of s (if needed for iDFT)","+9. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients","+10. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add)"," ","-```rust","-fn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(","-    d_g_bucket: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // 3 * skip_domain elements on device","-    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],             // E polynomial coefficients (computed on CPU)","-    skip_domain: usize,","-    large_uni_domain: usize,           // 2 * skip_domain","-    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // accumulator for s_0 coefficients","-) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>","-```","+Final: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB)."," ","-Implementation:","-1. **Prepare E coefficient buffer on GPU**: Flatten 3 E coefficient vectors into a single DeviceBuffer, padded to large_uni_domain × 3 elements. Upload via `to_device()`.","-2. **Prepare G coefficient buffer on GPU**: ","-   - Allocate a work buffer of size large_uni_domain × 3 EF elements","-   - Copy G evaluations from d_g_bucket into the first skip_domain positions of each 3-column group (with zero-padding to large_uni_domain)","-   - Run iDFT on the G portion (skip_domain-sized) to convert from evaluations to coefficients","-3. **Batch DFT both E and G** to the large_uni_domain evaluation domain","-4. **Pointwise multiply + accumulate** via a small CUDA kernel","-5. **iDFT the result** → product coefficients","-6. **Accumulate** into d_s0_accum via a simple element-wise add kernel","+### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA transpose kernel"," ","-For steps 2-3 and 5, use `batch_ntt_small` from `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/batch_ntt_small.rs`. Since NTT of EF elements with base-field twiddles decomposes into 4 independent base-field NTTs, we reinterpret `DeviceBuffer<EF>` as `DeviceBuffer<F>` with 4× the element count and set `cnt_blocks` accordingly.","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`"," ","-**Data layout for batch_ntt_small**:","+Add a small CUDA kernel to convert between AoS (FpExt layout) and SoA (4 × Fp layout):"," ","-The existing `batch_ntt_small` operates on contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` F elements. The EF elements in memory are stored as AoS: `[c0_0, c1_0, c2_0, c3_0, c0_1, c1_1, ...]`. For batch_ntt_small to treat each component independently, we need SoA layout.","+```cuda","+// AoS to SoA: input[n] = (c0,c1,c2,c3) → output[comp*n + i] for each comp","+__global__ void ef_aos_to_soa(","+    const uint32_t *input,   // n * 4 uint32_t (FpExt as 4 × Fp)","+    uint32_t *output,        // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA: all c0, then all c1, ...)","+    uint32_t n","+) {","+    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","+    if (i >= n) return;","+    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {","+        output[c * n + i] = input[i * 4 + c];","+    }","+}"," ","-Two options:","-- **Option A**: Add a small CUDA kernel to transpose AoS → SoA and back. This adds 2 kernel launches but is clean.","-- **Option B**: Write the NTT pipeline as a single fused CUDA kernel using `device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev` (from `device_ntt.cuh`) which operates within shared memory. Process each EF component sequentially within the kernel. This avoids the AoS/SoA issue entirely.","+// SoA to AoS: reverse of above","+__global__ void ef_soa_to_aos(","+    const uint32_t *input,   // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA)","+    uint32_t *output,        // n * 4 uint32_t (AoS, FpExt)","+    uint32_t n","+) {","+    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","+    if (i >= n) return;","+    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {","+        output[i * 4 + c] = input[c * n + i];","+    }","+}","+```"," ","-**Choose Option B** — a single fused kernel avoids layout issues and reduces kernel launch overhead:","+Grid: `(ceil(n/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For n=768 (3 × skip_domain): 3 blocks. Trivial."," ","-### Step 2: Fused CUDA kernel for NTT polynomial product-sum","+Add C launchers and Rust FFI wrappers in the corresponding files."," ","+### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel","+"," **File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`"," ","-Add kernel `stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel`:","-"," ```cuda","-// Each block processes one bucket.","-// Threads: skip_domain threads for iDFT, large_uni_domain threads for DFT.","-// Launch with large_uni_domain threads per block, num_active_buckets blocks.","-__global__ void stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel(","-    const FpExt *g_buckets,     // [num_buckets][3 * skip_domain]","-    const FpExt *e_buckets,     // [num_buckets][3 * large_uni_domain] (zero-padded coeffs)","-    FpExt *s0_out,              // [large_uni_domain] (accumulated across buckets)","-    uint32_t skip_domain,       // 1 << l_skip (e.g., 256)","-    uint32_t large_uni_domain,  // 2 * skip_domain (e.g., 512) ","-    uint32_t l_skip,            // e.g., 8","-    uint32_t l_large            // log2(large_uni_domain), e.g., 9","+// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]","+// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)","+// Output s is AoS FpExt format","+__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(","+    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]","+    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]","+    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]","+    uint32_t domain_size"," ) {","-    // Shared memory: 3 * large_uni_domain FpExt for G work + large_uni_domain FpExt for result","-    extern __shared__ FpExt smem[];","-    FpExt *g_smem = smem;                                // [3][large_uni_domain]","-    FpExt *s_smem = smem + 3 * large_uni_domain;         // [large_uni_domain]","-    ","-    uint32_t tid = threadIdx.x;","-    uint32_t bucket = blockIdx.x;","-    ","-    // 1. Load G evaluations into shared memory, zero-pad to large_uni_domain","-    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {","-        if (tid < skip_domain) {","-            g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid] = g_buckets[bucket * 3 * skip_domain + gi * skip_domain + tid];","-        } else {","-            g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid] = FpExt::zero();","-        }","+    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","+    if (j >= domain_size) return;","+    FpExt acc = FpExt::zero();","+    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {","+        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];","     }","-    __syncthreads();","-    ","-    // 2. iDFT each G vector (skip_domain points) → coefficients","-    //    Then DFT (large_uni_domain points) → evaluations","-    //    For each of 4 Fp components of FpExt, run the device_ntt","-    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {","-        FpExt *g_vec = g_smem + gi * large_uni_domain;","-        // Process each Fp component independently","-        for (int comp = 0; comp < 4; comp++) {","-            // Extract component into temp buffer, run NTT, write back","-            // ... (using device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev for iDFT on skip_domain)","-            // ... (using device_ntt::ntt_bitrev_to_natural for DFT on large_uni_domain)","-        }","-    }","-    __syncthreads();","-    ","-    // 3. DFT E coefficients (already in evaluation domain if pre-DFT'd on CPU)","-    //    OR: load E coefficients and DFT in shared memory (same as G)","-    ","-    // 4. Pointwise multiply: s[j] = sum_i E_i[j] * G_i[j]","-    FpExt s_val = FpExt::zero();","-    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {","-        FpExt e_val = e_buckets[bucket * 3 * large_uni_domain + gi * large_uni_domain + tid];","-        s_val += e_val * g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid];","-    }","-    s_smem[tid] = s_val;","-    __syncthreads();","-    ","-    // 5. iDFT of s → product coefficients","-    for (int comp = 0; comp < 4; comp++) {","-        // Run iDFT on comp-th component of s_smem","-    }","-    __syncthreads();","-    ","-    // 6. Atomic add to global s0_out","-    atomicAddExt(&s0_out[tid], s_smem[tid]);","+    s_out[j] = acc;"," }"," ```"," ","-**Shared memory**: 4 × large_uni_domain × sizeof(FpExt) = 4 × 512 × 16 = 32KB per block. Well within the 128KB limit.","+Grid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks."," ","-**Thread count**: large_uni_domain = 512 threads per block. For the iDFT of skip_domain=256, only the first 256 threads are active.","+### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel"," ","-**NTT within kernel**: Use the existing `device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true, needs_shmem=true>` for iDFT and `device_ntt::ntt_bitrev_to_natural<intt=false, needs_shmem=true>` for DFT. These operate on Fp values in shared memory. We process each of 4 Fp components of FpExt sequentially by extracting components into a shared Fp buffer, running the NTT, and writing back.","+```cuda","+// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len","+__global__ void ef_accumulate(","+    FpExt *accum,","+    const FpExt *addend,","+    uint32_t len","+) {","+    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","+    if (i >= len) return;","+    accum[i] += addend[i];","+}","+```"," ","-**Complication**: The `device_ntt` functions use `Fp` shared memory buffers, but our data is `FpExt`. We need a temporary `Fp` shared memory buffer of size `large_uni_domain` to hold one component at a time. This adds another 512 × 4 = 2KB. Total shared memory: ~34KB.","+No atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream."," ","-### Step 3: Rust FFI and launcher","+### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers"," "," **File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`"," ","-Add extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper for the new kernel.","+Add extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:","+- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`","+- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`","+- `ef_accumulate`"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`","+### Step 5: GPU pipeline function"," ","-Add C launcher function `_stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product` that computes grid/block dims and launches the kernel.","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`"," ","-### Step 4: Modify `reconstruct_s0_from_g`","+Add `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`","+```rust","+fn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(","+    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device","+    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)","+    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip","+    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain","+    l_skip: usize,","+    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements","+    // Pre-allocated work buffers:","+    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements","+    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements","+    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements","+    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements","+) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>","+```"," ","-Replace the body of `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):","+Implementation:","+1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`","+   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements"," ","-1. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients for all active buckets (unchanged — fast, ~100µs total)","-2. **CPU**: For n<0 buckets, compute E × indicator polynomial (unchanged — uses CPU poly_multiply_ntt, ~1ms total)","-3. **CPU**: DFT the E coefficients to evaluation domain (large_uni_domain points). This pre-computes E_evals so the GPU kernel only needs to load them. ~2ms CPU time.","-4. **CPU→GPU**: Upload pre-DFT'd E coefficients for all active buckets. Concatenated into one buffer. (~100KB, negligible)","-5. **GPU→GPU**: Collect d_g_pos and active d_g_neg buffers into a contiguous buffer (or use a pointer array). Track active bucket count and indices.","-6. **GPU**: Launch `stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel` with num_active_buckets blocks","-7. **GPU→CPU**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` — single sync for 511 EF elements (~8KB)","-8. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly","+2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`","+   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements","+   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order"," ","-**Active bucket detection**: During the per-trace kernel loop (lines 504-549), maintain a `BTreeSet<i8>` or bitmask of which n values were encountered. This tells us which buckets are active without requiring D2H to check for zeros.","+3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`","+   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block."," ","-### Step 5: Pre-DFT E on CPU","+4. **Bit-reverse + zero-pad**: Copy d_g_aos_padded into the first skip_domain positions of a 3 × large_uni_domain buffer. The bit-reversal from step 2 needs to be undone. **Since batch_ntt_small handles bit-reversal internally** (the kernel writes `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = result`), the output IS in natural order. So we just copy and zero-pad."," ","-To avoid running DFT of E coefficients on GPU (which would require additional kernel complexity), pre-DFT the E coefficients on CPU. Each E polynomial has ≤ skip_domain coefficients. DFT to large_uni_domain evaluation points:","+   Actually, let me clarify. Looking at `batch_ntt_small.cu` — the kernel `batch_ntt_small_kernel` at the end writes: `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. So the output is bit-reversed per block. We need to check whether `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order or bit-reversed output."," ","-```rust","-let e_evals: Vec<EF> = {","-    let mut padded = vec![EF::ZERO; large_uni_domain];","-    padded[..e_coeffs.len()].copy_from_slice(e_coeffs);","-    dft.dft(padded)","-};","-```","+   Reading the kernel source: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<INTT>` produces bit-reversed output. Then the kernel writes `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. Since `rev_len` reverses the bit pattern and `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` produces bit-reversed output, applying `rev_len` to the index gives natural-order output in global memory. **So the output of batch_ntt_small is in natural order.**"," ","-For 3 E polynomials × 9 buckets = 27 DFTs of 512-element EF vectors on CPU. At ~92µs each: ~2.5ms. This is ADDITIONAL CPU work compared to current (which does DFT as part of ntt_multiply_and_add). Net: the E DFT time stays similar, we just move it earlier.","+   → Zero-pad: copy 3 × skip_domain coefficients into positions 0..skip_domain of each 3 × large_uni_domain segment, zeros in skip_domain..large_uni_domain."," ","-**GPU kernel simplification**: With pre-DFT'd E, the GPU kernel only needs to:","-1. iDFT G evaluations → coefficients (skip_domain NTT)","-2. Pad to large_uni_domain","-3. DFT G coefficients → evaluations (large_uni_domain NTT)","-4. Pointwise multiply with pre-DFT'd E","-5. iDFT product → coefficients (large_uni_domain NTT)","+5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`"," ","-This is 3 NTT operations per G vector × 3 G vectors = 9 NTT operations per bucket, plus one iDFT for the product = 10 NTT operations per bucket.","+6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)"," ","+7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:","+   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G","+   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E","+","+8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply","+","+9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`","+","+10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT","+","+11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`","+","+12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt","+","+13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`","+","+### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`","+","+Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):","+","+1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.","+","+2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):","+   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each","+   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements","+   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements","+   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)","+","+3. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):","+   a. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients (eq_uni_poly, indicator multiply) — unchanged from current","+   b. **CPU**: Pad E coefficients to large_uni_domain, arrange in flat [3 × large_uni_domain] EF array","+   c. **CPU→GPU**: Upload E coefficients as EF via `to_device()`","+   d. **GPU**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, e_coeffs, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`","+","+4. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements","+5. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly","+","+### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL","+","+At the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.","+"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Mathematical equivalence**: The GPU NTT pipeline computes the same linear operation as the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add`. The NTT of EF elements with base-field twiddles decomposes into 4 independent base-field NTTs. The pointwise EF multiplication is faithful.","+1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness."," ","-2. **Accumulated result**: `s_0_coeffs` after all buckets must equal the current CPU-computed result. Use atomic adds across blocks (one block per bucket).","+2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation."," ","-3. **Skip-domain constraint**: The device NTT supports sizes up to `2^MAX_NTT_LEVEL = 2^10 = 1024`. With l_skip ≤ 10 (skip_domain ≤ 1024) and large_uni_domain ≤ 2048, this is within bounds. For l_skip > 10, fall back to the CPU path.","+3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering)."," "," 4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged."," ","-5. **No APC 0 regression**: Stacked Reduction is APC-independent (same l_skip, same bucket structure). Expected improvement similar across all APC configs.","+5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:","-- **Primary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1095-1100ms)","-- **Component metric**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~60-65ms)","-- **Regression check**: APC 0 STARK excl trace (expected: slight improvement or no change)","+Run `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:","+- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)","+- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)","+- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)"," ","-Verify correctness: all proofs verify successfully.","+Verify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span."," ","-Profile with nsight to confirm elimination of the D2H copy pattern in the Stacked Reduction round 0 span.","-"," ## Rollback Criteria"," "," Revert if any of:"," 1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms"," 2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace"," 3. Proof verification fails at any APC config","-4. l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10) at runtime — would need fallback path"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n\n## Goal\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using batched device NTT primitives. The current CPU pipeline performs per-bucket D2H transfers (each syncing the stream), followed by serial iDFT / DFT / pointwise-multiply / iDFT operations. Replacing this with GPU-side NTT eliminates ~8 redundant stream syncs and moves the NTT compute to GPU where it can run in parallel.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n\n1. **Per-trace GPU kernel loop** (lines 504-549): For each trace, calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` which launches the `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` and `final_reduce_block_sums<true>`. Results accumulate (+=) into per-bucket GPU buffers:\n   - `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n   - `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n\n2. **CPU reconstruction** (line 552): Calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641)\n\n### `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (stacked_reduction.rs:566-641)\n\n**Key dimensions** (with l_skip=8):\n- skip_domain = 256\n- s_0_deg = `sumcheck_round0_deg(8, 2)` = 2 × (256 − 1) = **510**\n- large_uni_domain = 512 (next_power_of_two(511))\n- Output: 511 EF coefficients\n\nFor each bucket (n≥0 and each active n<0):\n1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — **D2H + stream sync** (~200µs each)\n2. Check if all-zero (skip if so)\n3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU (eq_uni_poly, etc.)\n4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add`\n\n### `ntt_multiply_and_add` (stacked_reduction.rs:645-686)\n\nPer call (per bucket):\n1. **iDFT** of 3 G evaluation vectors (each 256 EF elements) → G coefficient vectors\n2. **Pad** both E and G coefficient vectors to 512 elements, transpose to column-major (512 × 3)\n3. **Batch DFT** of E and G matrices (3 columns × 512 rows each) → evaluation domain\n4. **Pointwise multiply**: s_eval[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j] (EF multiply at each of 512 points)\n5. **iDFT** of 512-element result → product coefficients\n6. **Accumulate** into s_0_coeffs\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nTotal across both APC 300 segments:\n- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n- Per-bucket NTT work: ~8-10 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements per bucket × ~9 active buckets per segment\n- CPU NTT of 512 EF elements: each involves 4 independent BabyBear NTTs (from extension field decomposition). Each 512-element NTT ≈ 512 × 9 × 20ns = 92µs. With ~60-80 NTTs total per segment: **~6-8ms** per segment\n- Total CPU reconstruction overhead per segment: **~8-12ms**\n- Total across 2 segments: **~16-24ms**\n\nNon-kernel overhead in Stacked Reduction: 74ms − 58ms (GPU kernels) = **~16ms**, consistent with the estimate above.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Step 1: Add GPU NTT pipeline function for polynomial product-sum\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd a new function `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add` that replaces the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` by doing the NTT pipeline on GPU:\n\n```rust\nfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n    d_g_bucket: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // 3 * skip_domain elements on device\n    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],             // E polynomial coefficients (computed on CPU)\n    skip_domain: usize,\n    large_uni_domain: usize,           // 2 * skip_domain\n    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // accumulator for s_0 coefficients\n) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n```\n\nImplementation:\n1. **Prepare E coefficient buffer on GPU**: Flatten 3 E coefficient vectors into a single DeviceBuffer, padded to large_uni_domain × 3 elements. Upload via `to_device()`.\n2. **Prepare G coefficient buffer on GPU**: \n   - Allocate a work buffer of size large_uni_domain × 3 EF elements\n   - Copy G evaluations from d_g_bucket into the first skip_domain positions of each 3-column group (with zero-padding to large_uni_domain)\n   - Run iDFT on the G portion (skip_domain-sized) to convert from evaluations to coefficients\n3. **Batch DFT both E and G** to the large_uni_domain evaluation domain\n4. **Pointwise multiply + accumulate** via a small CUDA kernel\n5. **iDFT the result** → product coefficients\n6. **Accumulate** into d_s0_accum via a simple element-wise add kernel\n\nFor steps 2-3 and 5, use `batch_ntt_small` from `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/batch_ntt_small.rs`. Since NTT of EF elements with base-field twiddles decomposes into 4 independent base-field NTTs, we reinterpret `DeviceBuffer<EF>` as `DeviceBuffer<F>` with 4× the element count and set `cnt_blocks` accordingly.\n\n**Data layout for batch_ntt_small**:\n\nThe existing `batch_ntt_small` operates on contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` F elements. The EF elements in memory are stored as AoS: `[c0_0, c1_0, c2_0, c3_0, c0_1, c1_1, ...]`. For batch_ntt_small to treat each component independently, we need SoA layout.\n\nTwo options:\n- **Option A**: Add a small CUDA kernel to transpose AoS → SoA and back. This adds 2 kernel launches but is clean.\n- **Option B**: Write the NTT pipeline as a single fused CUDA kernel using `device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev` (from `device_ntt.cuh`) which operates within shared memory. Process each EF component sequentially within the kernel. This avoids the AoS/SoA issue entirely.\n\n**Choose Option B** — a single fused kernel avoids layout issues and reduces kernel launch overhead:\n\n### Step 2: Fused CUDA kernel for NTT polynomial product-sum\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd kernel `stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n// Each block processes one bucket.\n// Threads: skip_domain threads for iDFT, large_uni_domain threads for DFT.\n// Launch with large_uni_domain threads per block, num_active_buckets blocks.\n__global__ void stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel(\n    const FpExt *g_buckets,     // [num_buckets][3 * skip_domain]\n    const FpExt *e_buckets,     // [num_buckets][3 * large_uni_domain] (zero-padded coeffs)\n    FpExt *s0_out,              // [large_uni_domain] (accumulated across buckets)\n    uint32_t skip_domain,       // 1 << l_skip (e.g., 256)\n    uint32_t large_uni_domain,  // 2 * skip_domain (e.g., 512) \n    uint32_t l_skip,            // e.g., 8\n    uint32_t l_large            // log2(large_uni_domain), e.g., 9\n) {\n    // Shared memory: 3 * large_uni_domain FpExt for G work + large_uni_domain FpExt for result\n    extern __shared__ FpExt smem[];\n    FpExt *g_smem = smem;                                // [3][large_uni_domain]\n    FpExt *s_smem = smem + 3 * large_uni_domain;         // [large_uni_domain]\n    \n    uint32_t tid = threadIdx.x;\n    uint32_t bucket = blockIdx.x;\n    \n    // 1. Load G evaluations into shared memory, zero-pad to large_uni_domain\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {\n        if (tid < skip_domain) {\n            g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid] = g_buckets[bucket * 3 * skip_domain + gi * skip_domain + tid];\n        } else {\n            g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid] = FpExt::zero();\n        }\n    }\n    __syncthreads();\n    \n    // 2. iDFT each G vector (skip_domain points) → coefficients\n    //    Then DFT (large_uni_domain points) → evaluations\n    //    For each of 4 Fp components of FpExt, run the device_ntt\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {\n        FpExt *g_vec = g_smem + gi * large_uni_domain;\n        // Process each Fp component independently\n        for (int comp = 0; comp < 4; comp++) {\n            // Extract component into temp buffer, run NTT, write back\n            // ... (using device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev for iDFT on skip_domain)\n            // ... (using device_ntt::ntt_bitrev_to_natural for DFT on large_uni_domain)\n        }\n    }\n    __syncthreads();\n    \n    // 3. DFT E coefficients (already in evaluation domain if pre-DFT'd on CPU)\n    //    OR: load E coefficients and DFT in shared memory (same as G)\n    \n    // 4. Pointwise multiply: s[j] = sum_i E_i[j] * G_i[j]\n    FpExt s_val = FpExt::zero();\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {\n        FpExt e_val = e_buckets[bucket * 3 * large_uni_domain + gi * large_uni_domain + tid];\n        s_val += e_val * g_smem[gi * large_uni_domain + tid];\n    }\n    s_smem[tid] = s_val;\n    __syncthreads();\n    \n    // 5. iDFT of s → product coefficients\n    for (int comp = 0; comp < 4; comp++) {\n        // Run iDFT on comp-th component of s_smem\n    }\n    __syncthreads();\n    \n    // 6. Atomic add to global s0_out\n    atomicAddExt(&s0_out[tid], s_smem[tid]);\n}\n```\n\n**Shared memory**: 4 × large_uni_domain × sizeof(FpExt) = 4 × 512 × 16 = 32KB per block. Well within the 128KB limit.\n\n**Thread count**: large_uni_domain = 512 threads per block. For the iDFT of skip_domain=256, only the first 256 threads are active.\n\n**NTT within kernel**: Use the existing `device_ntt::ntt_natural_to_bitrev<intt=true, needs_shmem=true>` for iDFT and `device_ntt::ntt_bitrev_to_natural<intt=false, needs_shmem=true>` for DFT. These operate on Fp values in shared memory. We process each of 4 Fp components of FpExt sequentially by extracting components into a shared Fp buffer, running the NTT, and writing back.\n\n**Complication**: The `device_ntt` functions use `Fp` shared memory buffers, but our data is `FpExt`. We need a temporary `Fp` shared memory buffer of size `large_uni_domain` to hold one component at a time. This adds another 512 × 4 = 2KB. Total shared memory: ~34KB.\n\n### Step 3: Rust FFI and launcher\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper for the new kernel.\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd C launcher function `_stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product` that computes grid/block dims and launches the kernel.\n\n### Step 4: Modify `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nReplace the body of `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n\n1. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients for all active buckets (unchanged — fast, ~100µs total)\n2. **CPU**: For n<0 buckets, compute E × indicator polynomial (unchanged — uses CPU poly_multiply_ntt, ~1ms total)\n3. **CPU**: DFT the E coefficients to evaluation domain (large_uni_domain points). This pre-computes E_evals so the GPU kernel only needs to load them. ~2ms CPU time.\n4. **CPU→GPU**: Upload pre-DFT'd E coefficients for all active buckets. Concatenated into one buffer. (~100KB, negligible)\n5. **GPU→GPU**: Collect d_g_pos and active d_g_neg buffers into a contiguous buffer (or use a pointer array). Track active bucket count and indices.\n6. **GPU**: Launch `stacked_reduction_ntt_poly_product_kernel` with num_active_buckets blocks\n7. **GPU→CPU**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` — single sync for 511 EF elements (~8KB)\n8. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n\n**Active bucket detection**: During the per-trace kernel loop (lines 504-549), maintain a `BTreeSet<i8>` or bitmask of which n values were encountered. This tells us which buckets are active without requiring D2H to check for zeros.\n\n### Step 5: Pre-DFT E on CPU\n\nTo avoid running DFT of E coefficients on GPU (which would require additional kernel complexity), pre-DFT the E coefficients on CPU. Each E polynomial has ≤ skip_domain coefficients. DFT to large_uni_domain evaluation points:\n\n```rust\nlet e_evals: Vec<EF> = {\n    let mut padded = vec![EF::ZERO; large_uni_domain];\n    padded[..e_coeffs.len()].copy_from_slice(e_coeffs);\n    dft.dft(padded)\n};\n```\n\nFor 3 E polynomials × 9 buckets = 27 DFTs of 512-element EF vectors on CPU. At ~92µs each: ~2.5ms. This is ADDITIONAL CPU work compared to current (which does DFT as part of ntt_multiply_and_add). Net: the E DFT time stays similar, we just move it earlier.\n\n**GPU kernel simplification**: With pre-DFT'd E, the GPU kernel only needs to:\n1. iDFT G evaluations → coefficients (skip_domain NTT)\n2. Pad to large_uni_domain\n3. DFT G coefficients → evaluations (large_uni_domain NTT)\n4. Pointwise multiply with pre-DFT'd E\n5. iDFT product → coefficients (large_uni_domain NTT)\n\nThis is 3 NTT operations per G vector × 3 G vectors = 9 NTT operations per bucket, plus one iDFT for the product = 10 NTT operations per bucket.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Mathematical equivalence**: The GPU NTT pipeline computes the same linear operation as the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add`. The NTT of EF elements with base-field twiddles decomposes into 4 independent base-field NTTs. The pointwise EF multiplication is faithful.\n\n2. **Accumulated result**: `s_0_coeffs` after all buckets must equal the current CPU-computed result. Use atomic adds across blocks (one block per bucket).\n\n3. **Skip-domain constraint**: The device NTT supports sizes up to `2^MAX_NTT_LEVEL = 2^10 = 1024`. With l_skip ≤ 10 (skip_domain ≤ 1024) and large_uni_domain ≤ 2048, this is within bounds. For l_skip > 10, fall back to the CPU path.\n\n4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n\n5. **No APC 0 regression**: Stacked Reduction is APC-independent (same l_skip, same bucket structure). Expected improvement similar across all APC configs.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n- **Primary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1095-1100ms)\n- **Component metric**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~60-65ms)\n- **Regression check**: APC 0 STARK excl trace (expected: slight improvement or no change)\n\nVerify correctness: all proofs verify successfully.\n\nProfile with nsight to confirm elimination of the D2H copy pattern in the Stacked Reduction round 0 span.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if any of:\n1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n4. l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10) at runtime — would need fallback path\n"}}
