{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDZTFdWtXFQzH9n2NQLcwT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n10\t\n11\tPer-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:\n12\t- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n13\t- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n14\t\n15\tThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:\n16\t1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)\n17\t2. Check all-zero, skip if so\n18\t3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU\n19\t4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n20\t5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline\n21\t\n22\t### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)\n23\t- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512\n24\t- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients\n25\t- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements\n26\t- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)\n27\t\n28\t### Why it's slow\n29\t- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n30\t- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**\n31\t- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**\n32\t\n33\t## Changes\n34\t\n35\t### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline\n36\t\n37\tReplace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity.\n38\t\n39\tPipeline per bucket:\n40\t1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)\n41\t2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)\n42\t3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients\n43\t4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain\n44\t5. CPU: Compute E coefficients, DFT to evaluation domain, upload as SoA Fp\n45\t6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals\n46\t7. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n47\t8. GPU: AoS → SoA of s (if needed for iDFT)\n48\t9. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients\n49\t10. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add)\n50\t\n51\tFinal: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB).\n52\t\n53\t### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA transpose kernel\n54\t\n55\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n56\t\n57\tAdd a small CUDA kernel to convert between AoS (FpExt layout) and SoA (4 × Fp layout):\n58\t\n59\t```cuda\n60\t// AoS to SoA: input[n] = (c0,c1,c2,c3) → output[comp*n + i] for each comp\n61\t__global__ void ef_aos_to_soa(\n62\t    const uint32_t *input,   // n * 4 uint32_t (FpExt as 4 × Fp)\n63\t    uint32_t *output,        // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA: all c0, then all c1, ...)\n64\t    uint32_t n\n65\t) {\n66\t    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n67\t    if (i >= n) return;\n68\t    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n69\t        output[c * n + i] = input[i * 4 + c];\n70\t    }\n71\t}\n72\t\n73\t// SoA to AoS: reverse of above\n74\t__global__ void ef_soa_to_aos(\n75\t    const uint32_t *input,   // 4 * n uint32_t (SoA)\n76\t    uint32_t *output,        // n * 4 uint32_t (AoS, FpExt)\n77\t    uint32_t n\n78\t) {\n79\t    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n80\t    if (i >= n) return;\n81\t    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n82\t        output[i * 4 + c] = input[c * n + i];\n83\t    }\n84\t}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tGrid: `(ceil(n/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For n=768 (3 × skip_domain): 3 blocks. Trivial.\n88\t\n89\tAdd C launchers and Rust FFI wrappers in the corresponding files.\n90\t\n91\t### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel\n92\t\n93\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n94\t\n95\t```cuda\n96\t// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n97\t// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)\n98\t// Output s is AoS FpExt format\n99\t__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(\n100\t    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n101\t    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n102\t    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]\n103\t    uint32_t domain_size\n104\t) {\n105\t    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n106\t    if (j >= domain_size) return;\n107\t    FpExt acc = FpExt::zero();\n108\t    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n109\t        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];\n110\t    }\n111\t    s_out[j] = acc;\n112\t}\n113\t```\n114\t\n115\tGrid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks.\n116\t\n117\t### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel\n118\t\n119\t```cuda\n120\t// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len\n121\t__global__ void ef_accumulate(\n122\t    FpExt *accum,\n123\t    const FpExt *addend,\n124\t    uint32_t len\n125\t) {\n126\t    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n127\t    if (i >= len) return;\n128\t    accum[i] += addend[i];\n129\t}\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\tNo atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream.\n133\t\n134\t### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers\n135\t\n136\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n137\t\n138\tAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:\n139\t- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`\n140\t- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`\n141\t- `ef_accumulate`\n142\t\n143\t### Step 5: GPU pipeline function\n144\t\n145\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n146\t\n147\tAdd `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:\n148\t\n149\t```rust\n150\tfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n151\t    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device\n152\t    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)\n153\t    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip\n154\t    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain\n155\t    l_skip: usize,\n156\t    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements\n157\t    // Pre-allocated work buffers:\n158\t    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n159\t    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n160\t    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements\n161\t    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements\n162\t) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n163\t```\n164\t\n165\tImplementation:\n166\t1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`\n167\t   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements\n168\t\n169\t2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`\n170\t   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements\n171\t   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order\n172\t\n173\t3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`\n174\t   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block.\n175\t\n176\t4. **Bit-reverse + zero-pad**: Copy d_g_aos_padded into the first skip_domain positions of a 3 × large_uni_domain buffer. The bit-reversal from step 2 needs to be undone. **Since batch_ntt_small handles bit-reversal internally** (the kernel writes `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)] = result`), the output IS in natural order. So we just copy and zero-pad.\n177\t\n178\t   Actually, let me clarify. Looking at `batch_ntt_small.cu` — the kernel `batch_ntt_small_kernel` at the end writes: `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. So the output is bit-reversed per block. We need to check whether `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order or bit-reversed output.\n179\t\n180\t   Reading the kernel source: `ntt_natural_to_bitrev<INTT>` produces bit-reversed output. Then the kernel writes `d_buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip) * cnt_blocks + block_idx] = this_thread_value`. Since `rev_len` reverses the bit pattern and `ntt_natural_to_bitrev` produces bit-reversed output, applying `rev_len` to the index gives natural-order output in global memory. **So the output of batch_ntt_small is in natural order.**\n181\t\n182\t   → Zero-pad: copy 3 × skip_domain coefficients into positions 0..skip_domain of each 3 × large_uni_domain segment, zeros in skip_domain..large_uni_domain.\n183\t\n184\t5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`\n185\t\n186\t6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)\n187\t\n188\t7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:\n189\t   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G\n190\t   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E\n191\t\n192\t8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply\n193\t\n194\t9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`\n195\t\n196\t10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT\n197\t\n198\t11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`\n199\t\n200\t12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt\n201\t\n202\t13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`\n203\t\n204\t### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n205\t\n206\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n207\t\n208\tReplace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n209\t\n210\t1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.\n211\t\n212\t2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):\n213\t   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each\n214\t   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements\n215\t   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements\n216\t   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)\n217\t\n218\t3. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):\n219\t   a. **CPU**: Compute E polynomial coefficients (eq_uni_poly, indicator multiply) — unchanged from current\n220\t   b. **CPU**: Pad E coefficients to large_uni_domain, arrange in flat [3 × large_uni_domain] EF array\n221\t   c. **CPU→GPU**: Upload E coefficients as EF via `to_device()`\n222\t   d. **GPU**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, e_coeffs, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`\n223\t\n224\t4. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements\n225\t5. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n226\t\n227\t### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL\n228\t\n229\tAt the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.\n230\t\n231\t## Invariants\n232\t\n233\t1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness.\n234\t\n235\t2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation.\n236\t\n237\t3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering).\n238\t\n239\t4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n240\t\n241\t5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block.\n242\t\n243\t## Measurement Plan\n244\t\n245\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n246\t- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)\n247\t- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)\n248\t- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)\n249\t\n250\tVerify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span.\n251\t\n252\t## Rollback Criteria\n253\t\n254\tRevert if any of:\n255\t1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n256\t2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n257\t3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n258\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013TAmRjFGtMgqpckjApbrer","session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"64eef823-9662-411d-99e7-98fdc9adad87","timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:06:33.719Z"}
