{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014JvkyAgSBhEaYQJqKxJqvy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n10\t\n11\tPer-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:\n12\t- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n13\t- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n14\t\n15\tThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:\n16\t1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)\n17\t2. Check all-zero, skip if so\n18\t3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU\n19\t4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n20\t5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline\n21\t\n22\t### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)\n23\t- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512\n24\t- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients\n25\t- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements\n26\t- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)\n27\t\n28\t### Why it's slow\n29\t- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n30\t- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**\n31\t- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**\n32\t\n33\t## Changes\n34\t\n35\t### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline\n36\t\n37\tReplace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity.\n38\t\n39\tPipeline per bucket:\n40\t1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)\n41\t2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)\n42\t3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients\n43\t4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain\n44\t5. E evaluations pre-computed and pre-uploaded (see Step 6 in orchestration)\n45\t6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals\n46\t7. GPU: SoA→AoS of G via `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec`\n47\t8. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n48\t9. GPU: AoS→SoA of s via `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix`\n49\t10. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients\n50\t11. GPU: SoA→AoS of result via `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec`\n51\t12. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add, no atomics — same stream)\n52\t\n53\tFinal: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB).\n54\t\n55\t### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA conversion (reuse existing kernels)\n56\t\n57\tReuse existing codebase primitives for AoS↔SoA conversion:\n58\t- **AoS→SoA (EF → 4×Fp)**: `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs:145`\n59\t- **SoA→AoS (4×Fp → EF)**: `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/poly.rs:56`\n60\t\n61\tThese are already used by the WHIR prover (`crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:100-153`) for exactly this pattern: convert EF to Fp SoA, run `batch_ntt_small`, convert back. No new kernels needed for AoS↔SoA.\n62\t\n63\t### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel\n64\t\n65\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n66\t\n67\t```cuda\n68\t// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n69\t// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)\n70\t// Output s is AoS FpExt format\n71\t__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(\n72\t    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n73\t    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n74\t    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]\n75\t    uint32_t domain_size\n76\t) {\n77\t    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n78\t    if (j >= domain_size) return;\n79\t    FpExt acc;\n80\t    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n81\t        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];\n82\t    }\n83\t    s_out[j] = acc;\n84\t}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tGrid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks.\n88\t\n89\t### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel\n90\t\n91\t```cuda\n92\t// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len\n93\t__global__ void ef_accumulate(\n94\t    FpExt *accum,\n95\t    const FpExt *addend,\n96\t    uint32_t len\n97\t) {\n98\t    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n99\t    if (i >= len) return;\n100\t    accum[i] += addend[i];\n101\t}\n102\t```\n103\t\n104\tNo atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream.\n105\t\n106\t### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers\n107\t\n108\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n109\t\n110\tAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:\n111\t- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`\n112\t- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`\n113\t- `ef_accumulate`\n114\t\n115\t### Step 5: GPU pipeline function\n116\t\n117\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n118\t\n119\tAdd `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:\n120\t\n121\t```rust\n122\tfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n123\t    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device\n124\t    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)\n125\t    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip\n126\t    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain\n127\t    l_skip: usize,\n128\t    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements\n129\t    // Pre-allocated work buffers:\n130\t    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n131\t    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n132\t    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements\n133\t    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements\n134\t) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n135\t```\n136\t\n137\tImplementation:\n138\t1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`\n139\t   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements\n140\t\n141\t2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`\n142\t   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements\n143\t   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order\n144\t\n145\t3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`\n146\t   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block.\n147\t\n148\t4. **Zero-pad**: `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order output (confirmed by existing usage in `sumcheck.rs:200-234` where iDFT output feeds directly to `batch_expand_pad_wide` without bit-reversal). Use the existing `batch_expand_pad` kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs:234`) to zero-pad each skip_domain block to large_uni_domain.\n149\t\n150\t5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`\n151\t\n152\t6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)\n153\t\n154\t7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:\n155\t   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G\n156\t   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E\n157\t\n158\t8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply\n159\t\n160\t9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`\n161\t\n162\t10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT\n163\t\n164\t11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`\n165\t\n166\t12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt\n167\t\n168\t13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`\n169\t\n170\t### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n171\t\n172\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n173\t\n174\tReplace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n175\t\n176\t1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.\n177\t\n178\t2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):\n179\t   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each\n180\t   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements\n181\t   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements\n182\t   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)\n183\t\n184\t3. **Pre-compute ALL E evaluations on CPU** (before GPU loop):\n185\t   For each active bucket, compute E polynomial coefficients, DFT them to the evaluation domain (large_uni_domain points) on CPU via `Radix2BowersSerial::dft`, and store in a flat `Vec<EF>`. Upload ALL E evaluation data in a single `to_device()` call. This removes per-bucket CPU→GPU sync points and allows the GPU pipeline to run without CPU interruption.\n186\t\n187\t4. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):\n188\t   - **GPU only**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, d_e_evals_for_bucket, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`\n189\t   - No per-bucket CPU work or H2D transfers\n190\t\n191\t5. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements\n192\t6. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n193\t\n194\t### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL\n195\t\n196\tAt the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.\n197\t\n198\t## Invariants\n199\t\n200\t1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness.\n201\t\n202\t2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation.\n203\t\n204\t3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering).\n205\t\n206\t4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n207\t\n208\t5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block.\n209\t\n210\t## Measurement Plan\n211\t\n212\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n213\t- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)\n214\t- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)\n215\t- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)\n216\t\n217\tVerify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span.\n218\t\n219\t## Rollback Criteria\n220\t\n221\tRevert if any of:\n222\t1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n223\t2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n224\t3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n225\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fae41bbf-912e-42ee-9c72-17c79e3a14c7","uuid":"e1720c43-4e55-47af-b9c9-2fb78ae6778c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:12:42.582Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0830-gpu-stacked-reduction-poly-extraction/plan.md","content":"# Plan: GPU Stacked Reduction Polynomial Extraction\n\n## Goal\n\nMove the Stacked Reduction Round 0 polynomial reconstruction (`reconstruct_s0_from_g` + `ntt_multiply_and_add`) from CPU to GPU using a multi-kernel pipeline with the existing `batch_ntt_small` primitive. This eliminates per-bucket D2H + stream sync overhead (~3.6ms) and moves the NTT compute to GPU (~6-10ms CPU NTT → <1ms GPU NTT), for an estimated net ~10ms improvement on the 74ms Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry: `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` (crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:481-556)\n\nPer-trace GPU kernel loop accumulates G0, G1, G2 evaluations into bucket buffers:\n- `d_g_pos`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements (for n ≥ 0 traces)\n- `d_g_neg[k]`: 3 × skip_domain EF elements per bucket (for |n| = k+1, k in 0..l_skip)\n\nThen calls `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641) which for each active bucket:\n1. `d_g_bucket.to_host()` — D2H + stream sync (~200µs per bucket, ~9 buckets × 2 segments)\n2. Check all-zero, skip if so\n3. Compute E polynomial coefficients on CPU\n4. For n<0 buckets: multiply E by indicator polynomial via `poly_multiply_ntt`\n5. Call `ntt_multiply_and_add` — CPU iDFT/DFT/pointwise-multiply/iDFT pipeline\n\n### Key dimensions (l_skip=8)\n- skip_domain = 256, large_uni_domain = 512\n- s_0_deg = 2 × (256 − 1) = 510 → output has 511 coefficients\n- Each G bucket: 3 × 256 = 768 EF elements\n- Each E polynomial: ≤ 256 coefficients (padded to 512 for DFT)\n\n### Why it's slow\n- 9 D2H + stream sync per segment × 2 segments: ~18 × 200µs = **~3.6ms** from syncs\n- CPU NTT work per segment: ~60-80 NTTs of 256-512 EF elements = **~6-8ms**\n- Total addressable overhead: **~10-16ms**\n\n## Changes\n\n### Overview: Multi-kernel GPU pipeline\n\nReplace the CPU `ntt_multiply_and_add` with a GPU pipeline using existing `batch_ntt_small` and two small new CUDA kernels (AoS↔SoA transpose, pointwise multiply+sum). No fused kernel — each step is a separate kernel launch for correctness and simplicity.\n\nPipeline per bucket:\n1. G evaluations already on GPU (in d_g_pos / d_g_neg)\n2. GPU: AoS → SoA transpose (FpExt → 4 × Fp layout)\n3. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on Fp data — iDFT of G evals → G coefficients\n4. GPU: Zero-pad G from skip_domain to large_uni_domain\n5. E evaluations pre-computed and pre-uploaded (see Step 6 in orchestration)\n6. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=false)` on Fp data — DFT of G coefficients → G evals\n7. GPU: SoA→AoS of G via `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec`\n8. GPU: Pointwise EF multiply+sum kernel: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n9. GPU: AoS→SoA of s via `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix`\n10. GPU: `batch_ntt_small(is_intt=true)` on s — iDFT → product coefficients\n11. GPU: SoA→AoS of result via `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec`\n12. GPU: Accumulate into d_s0_accum (element-wise add, no atomics — same stream)\n\nFinal: single D2H of d_s0_accum (511 EF elements = ~8KB).\n\n### Step 1: AoS ↔ SoA conversion (reuse existing kernels)\n\nReuse existing codebase primitives for AoS↔SoA conversion:\n- **AoS→SoA (EF → 4×Fp)**: `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs:145`\n- **SoA→AoS (4×Fp → EF)**: `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/poly.rs:56`\n\nThese are already used by the WHIR prover (`crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:100-153`) for exactly this pattern: convert EF to Fp SoA, run `batch_ntt_small`, convert back. No new kernels needed for AoS↔SoA.\n\n### Step 2: Pointwise EF multiply-sum kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\n```cuda\n// Pointwise multiply 3 pairs of EF vectors and sum: s[j] = Σᵢ E_i[j] × G_i[j]\n// Input E and G are AoS FpExt format (already DFT'd to evaluation domain)\n// Output s is AoS FpExt format\n__global__ void ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(\n    const FpExt *e_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    const FpExt *g_evals,   // [3 × domain_size]\n    FpExt *s_out,           // [domain_size]\n    uint32_t domain_size\n) {\n    uint32_t j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (j >= domain_size) return;\n    FpExt acc;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        acc += e_evals[i * domain_size + j] * g_evals[i * domain_size + j];\n    }\n    s_out[j] = acc;\n}\n```\n\nGrid: `(ceil(domain_size/256), 1)`, Block: `(256, 1)`. For domain_size=512: 2 blocks.\n\n### Step 3: Element-wise EF accumulate kernel\n\n```cuda\n// s0_accum[i] += s_new[i] for i in 0..len\n__global__ void ef_accumulate(\n    FpExt *accum,\n    const FpExt *addend,\n    uint32_t len\n) {\n    uint32_t i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (i >= len) return;\n    accum[i] += addend[i];\n}\n```\n\nNo atomics needed — we process buckets sequentially on the same stream.\n\n### Step 4: Rust FFI wrappers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers for:\n- `ef_aos_to_soa` / `ef_soa_to_aos`\n- `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum`\n- `ef_accumulate`\n\n### Step 5: GPU pipeline function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add`:\n\n```rust\nfn gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(\n    d_g_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,      // 3 * skip_domain EF elements on device\n    e_coeffs: [&[EF]; 3],              // E polynomial coefficients (from CPU)\n    skip_domain: usize,                 // 1 << l_skip\n    large_uni_domain: usize,            // 2 * skip_domain\n    l_skip: usize,\n    d_s0_accum: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,  // accumulator, large_uni_domain elements\n    // Pre-allocated work buffers:\n    d_g_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_e_soa: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,      // 4 * 3 * large_uni_domain Fp elements\n    d_g_aos_padded: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, // 3 * large_uni_domain EF elements\n    d_s_work: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,    // large_uni_domain EF elements\n) -> Result<(), StackedReductionError>\n```\n\nImplementation:\n1. **AoS→SoA of G**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_evals, d_g_soa, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - d_g_soa now has 4 contiguous blocks of 3 × skip_domain Fp elements\n\n2. **iDFT of G (SoA)**: Call `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa, l_skip, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=true)`\n   - 12 blocks = 4 EF components × 3 G vectors, each 2^l_skip Fp elements\n   - After: d_g_soa contains G coefficients in SoA bit-reversed order\n\n3. **SoA→AoS of G coefficients**: Launch `ef_soa_to_aos(d_g_soa, d_g_aos_padded, 3 * skip_domain)`\n   - Note: `batch_ntt_small` writes output in bit-reversed order at global memory positions (it does the `buffer[rev_len(i, l_skip)]` assignment internally). So after SoA→AoS, the coefficients are in bit-reversed order within each 3 × skip_domain block.\n\n4. **Zero-pad**: `batch_ntt_small` produces natural-order output (confirmed by existing usage in `sumcheck.rs:200-234` where iDFT output feeds directly to `batch_expand_pad_wide` without bit-reversal). Use the existing `batch_expand_pad` kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs:234`) to zero-pad each skip_domain block to large_uni_domain.\n\n5. **AoS→SoA of padded G coefficients**: Launch `ef_aos_to_soa(d_g_padded, d_g_soa_large, 3 * large_uni_domain)`\n\n6. **Prepare E in SoA on GPU**: CPU computes E coefficients, pads to large_uni_domain, flattens 3 E polynomials, uploads as EF AoS. Then GPU AoS→SoA. (Alternatively, CPU does the SoA layout directly and uploads Fp data.)\n\n7. **DFT of G and E (SoA)**: Two calls to `batch_ntt_small`:\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_g_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of G\n   - `batch_ntt_small(d_e_soa_large, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=12, is_intt=false)` — DFT of E\n\n8. **SoA→AoS of G and E**: Convert back to FpExt format for the pointwise multiply\n\n9. **Pointwise multiply**: Launch `ef_pointwise_mul3_sum(d_e_aos, d_g_aos, d_s_work, large_uni_domain)`\n\n10. **AoS→SoA of s_work**: Convert for iDFT\n\n11. **iDFT of product (SoA)**: `batch_ntt_small(d_s_soa, l_skip+1, cnt_blocks=4, is_intt=true)`\n\n12. **SoA→AoS of result**: Convert back to FpExt\n\n13. **Accumulate**: Launch `ef_accumulate(d_s0_accum, d_s_work_aos, large_uni_domain)`\n\n### Step 6: Replace `reconstruct_s0_from_g`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nReplace `reconstruct_s0_from_g` (lines 566-641):\n\n1. **Track active buckets**: During the per-trace kernel loop (already in `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`), maintain a bitmask of active buckets based on the `n` value computed at line 511.\n\n2. **Pre-allocate work buffers** once (before the bucket loop):\n   - d_g_soa, d_e_soa: 4 × 3 × large_uni_domain Fp elements each\n   - d_g_aos_padded: 3 × large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s_work: large_uni_domain EF elements\n   - d_s0_accum: large_uni_domain EF elements (zero-initialized)\n\n3. **Pre-compute ALL E evaluations on CPU** (before GPU loop):\n   For each active bucket, compute E polynomial coefficients, DFT them to the evaluation domain (large_uni_domain points) on CPU via `Radix2BowersSerial::dft`, and store in a flat `Vec<EF>`. Upload ALL E evaluation data in a single `to_device()` call. This removes per-bucket CPU→GPU sync points and allows the GPU pipeline to run without CPU interruption.\n\n4. **For each active bucket** (n≥0, then each active n<0):\n   - **GPU only**: Call `gpu_ntt_multiply_and_add(d_g_bucket, d_e_evals_for_bucket, ..., d_s0_accum, ...)`\n   - No per-bucket CPU work or H2D transfers\n\n5. **Single D2H**: `d_s0_accum.to_host()` → 511 EF elements\n6. Truncate to s_0_deg+1 coefficients, return UnivariatePoly\n\n### Step 7: Guard for l_skip > MAX_NTT_LEVEL\n\nAt the top of the new `reconstruct_s0_from_g`, check `l_skip + 1 > MAX_NTT_LEVEL (10)`. If so, fall back to the existing CPU implementation (which remains as a private helper method). For the current l_skip=8, the GPU path is always taken.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Mathematical equivalence**: NTT of EF with base-field twiddles = 4 independent Fp NTTs. The `batch_ntt_small` kernel produces natural-order output (it applies bit-reversal internally). The pointwise multiply uses full EF multiplication (not component-wise), preserving correctness.\n\n2. **Buffer lifecycle**: Work buffers (d_g_soa, d_e_soa, etc.) are allocated before the bucket loop and reused across buckets. The d_s0_accum persists across buckets for accumulation.\n\n3. **Bucket ordering**: Buckets are processed sequentially on the same stream. No atomics needed — `ef_accumulate` runs after the previous bucket's pipeline completes (stream ordering).\n\n4. **No protocol change**: The output polynomial s_0, its degree, and the transcript interaction are unchanged.\n\n5. **batch_ntt_small contract**: It takes `cnt_blocks` contiguous blocks of `1 << l_skip` Fp elements, each independently NTT'd. The SoA layout ensures each Fp component of each G/E vector is a separate block.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Compare:\n- **Primary**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expected: 1110ms → ~1100ms)\n- **Component**: Stacked Reduction at APC 300 (expected: 74ms → ~64ms)\n- **Regression check**: APC 0 (expected: no regression or slight improvement)\n\nVerify correctness: all proofs verify. Profile with nsight to confirm D2H count reduction in the Stacked Reduction span.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if any of:\n1. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 improves by less than 5ms\n2. APC 0 regresses by more than 10ms on STARK excl trace\n3. Proof verification fails at any APC config\n","numLines":225,"startLine":1,"totalLines":225}}}
